\section{Introduction}

In contrast with textbook instantaneous projective measurements,  continuous measurements allow one to extract partial information about a quantum state without fully collapsing it~\cite{Landi2024}. 
In addition, continuous measurements allow for the implementation of feedback protocols, where the classical (noisy) data obtained from the measurements are fed back in real time to the system. 
Quantum continuous measurements have been experimentally realized across many quantum platforms, such as optical cavities~\cite{gleyzes2007}, quantum dots~\cite{gustavsson2006,bayer2025}, and superconducting circuits~\cite{vijay2011,murch2013,minev2019}.

From a theory perspective, these processes can be difficult to simulate, since they are described by stochastic master equations, which have to be sampled numerous times to extract the statistical behavior of the monitored system.
This motivates the search for deterministic solution methods that can encompass the ensemble-averaged response of the system. 
Traditionally, such methods have been largely restricted to what is known as current-based feedback, where at each step only the very last data point, called a current $z(t)$, is used to guide the feedback~\cite{wiseman2009}. 
In this paradigm the current $z(t)$ is a random variable and one could, for example, modify  the Hamiltonian based on the obtained value of $z$ at each step. 
More recently, this has been extended to charge-based feedback~\cite{Kewming2024}, where one uses instead the entire integrated current $N(t) = \int_0^t dt'~z(t')$. 

Both examples are actually particular cases of a low pass filter-based feedback, where one uses instead 
\begin{equation}
    D(t)=\int_{0}^t dt' \, \gamma e^{-\gamma(t-t')} z(t'),
\end{equation}
which recovers the current- and charge-resolved cases when $\gamma\to \infty$ and $\gamma\to 0$ respectively. 
Recently, a deterministic method for simulating feedback based on this kind of low pass filter was put forward in Ref.~\cite{annby-anderssonQuantumFokkerPlanckMaster2022}, for the case of the diffusive unraveling of the master equation.
In this formalism, in addition to the usual open system dynamics, an external agent is also continuously measuring a certain operator $A$ through a weak Gaussian measurement defined by Kraus operators
\begin{equation}
    K(z) = \left( \frac{2\lambda dt}{\pi}\right)^{1/4} e^{-\lambda dt(z-A)^2},
\end{equation}
with strength $\lambda$.
Their main result is the introduction of a signal-resolved density matrix $\rho_t(D)$, defined such that $\Tr\big\{\rho_t(D)\big\} = P(D)$ is the probability that the signal has a certain value $D$ at time $t$. 
As the authors have shown, $\rho_t(D)$ satisfies a Fokker-Planck master equation of the form 
\begin{eqnarray}
\partial_t \rho_t(D) &=& \mathcal{L}(D)  \rho_t(D) + \lambda \mathcal{D}\left[A\right]  \rho_t(D)-\gamma \partial_D \mathcal{C}_{A-D} \rho_t(D)/2 \nonumber \\ &+&\frac{\gamma^2}{8 \lambda} \partial_D^2 \rho_t(D),\label{FP_eq}
\end{eqnarray}
where $\mathcal{L}(D)=\mathcal{L}_0 + \sum_p f_p (D) \mathcal{L}_p$ is the Liouvillian which can in principle have any dependence on the signal $D$, $\mathcal{C}_X=\{X,\cdot\}$ is the anti-commutator with $X$ and $\mathcal{D}\left[A\right]=A \cdot A^\dagger - \frac{1}{2} \{A^\dagger A, \cdot\}$ is the Lindblad dissipator describing the dephasing back action due to measurement of the observable $A$. 

This type of master equation has since been used to study the generation of steady-state entanglement from thermal resources using feedback \cite{Diotallevi_2024}, the cooling of a particle in a harmonic trap \cite{desousa2024} and the exploration of Maxwell demons in quantum and classical mechanics \cite{annby2024maxwell}.
Eq.~\eqref{FP_eq} has also recently been generalized to the case of multiple detectors \cite{desousa2024}.

However, an outstanding question remains as to how to solve Eq.~\eqref{FP_eq} efficiently. 
One can view it as a system of coupled partial differential equations, of the diffusion type; one for each element of the density matrix. 
From an analytical point of view and, in fact, even numerically, solving Eq.~\eqref{FP_eq} will be quite challenging, even for simple systems such as a single qubit. 
The main goal of this paper is to put forward an efficient solution method. 
First, we will show that if there is no feedback present, the solution is greatly simplified. 
At first, the case without feedback might seem to defeat the entire purpose of the method. 
However, this is not true because the equation can still be used to find the signal probability $P(D)$, which is experimentally meaningful. 
For example, one might be interested in performing metrology with this signal; i.e., estimating a parameter $\mu$ from the signal stochastic data $D(t)$. 
We show that in the absence of feedback it is possible to efficiently compute the Fisher information of $P(D)$ using our method. 
This follows various recent papers investigating the Fisher information of the output current of a continuously monitored quantum system \cite{gammelmark2013,gammelmark2014,kiilerich2014,radaelli2024,smiga2023,ilias2022,yang2023}.
We also show how the results without feedback can be used to develop a perturbative solution method that allows one to efficiently include weak feedback.

\section{Formal framework}

Following the results in \cite{annby-anderssonQuantumFokkerPlanckMaster2022}, we start by expanding the density operator in terms of the generalized Hermite polynomials
\begin{eqnarray}
\rho_t(D) = \sum_{n=0}^{\infty} M_n \frac{\mathcal{H}_n(D)}{\sqrt{\sigma^n n!}} \frac{e^{-D^2/2\sigma}}{\sqrt{2 \pi \sigma}}, \label{ansatz}
\end{eqnarray}
with 
\begin{eqnarray}
\mathcal{H}_n(x) = \left(\frac{\sigma}{2}\right)^{n/2} H_n\left(\frac{x}{\sqrt{2 \sigma}}\right),
\end{eqnarray}
where $\mathcal{H}_n(x)$ are generalized Hermite polynomials,   $H_n(x)$ are standard physicist’s Hermite polynomials, and $M_n$ are Hermitian matrices of dimension $R$. We also defined the diffusion coefficient 
\begin{equation}
    \sigma = \frac{\gamma}{8\lambda}.
\end{equation}
The state is then fully determined by the matrices $M_n$. Later when we show how to numerically solve Eq.~\eqref{FP_eq}, this sum will be truncated to $N$ terms. In the following parts, we will outline the different quantities that can be directly computed from these matrices, in some cases with only the first few.

\subsection{Statistical moments of $P(D)$}

We start by defining the coefficients 
\begin{equation}
c_m=\Tr(M_m)= \int dD P(D)  \frac{\mathcal{H}_m(D)}{\sqrt{\sigma^m m!}}. 
\end{equation}
Note that $c_0=1$. The average of $P(D)$ is 
\begin{equation}
\avg{D}= \sqrt{\sigma} c_1.
\end{equation}
Similarly, the second moment $\avg{D^2}= \sqrt{2} \sigma  c_2+\sigma$.  This gives the variance as 
\begin{equation}
    \rm{Var}(D)= \sigma (1+ \sqrt{2}  c_2  -  c_1^2).
\end{equation}
More generally, the $q^{\rm th}$ moment is
\begin{eqnarray}
    \avg{D^q} = \sum_n c_n J_n(q),
    \qquad 
    J_n(q)= \int dD D^q \frac{\mathcal{H}_n(D)}{\sqrt{\sigma^n n!}}  \frac{e^{-D^2/2\sigma}}{\sqrt{2 \pi \sigma}}. \nonumber \\
\end{eqnarray}
This integral can be solved recursively using
\begin{eqnarray}
    J_n(q) &=& \sqrt{\sigma (n+1)} J_{n+1}(q-1)+\sqrt{n \sigma} J_{n-1}(q-1), \\
    J_n(0) &=& \delta_{n,0}.
\end{eqnarray}
Since $J_n(q)$ is only nonzero for $n \leq q$, only the first $q+1$ coefficients are needed to compute $\avg{D^q}$.

\subsection{Reconstruction of $P(D)$}
The characteristic function of $P(D)$ is
\begin{eqnarray}
    \avg{e^{i K D}}= e^{-K^2 \sigma/ 2} \sum_m c_m \frac{ (i K)^m \sigma^{m/2}}{\sqrt{m!}}.
\end{eqnarray}
This is quite useful, as reconstructing $P(D)$ directly using the trace of Eq. \eqref{ansatz} leads to poor convergence due to fast oscillating terms. A more efficient strategy is to construct this characteristic function and filter out very high frequencies when inverse Fourier transforming back to probability space.

\subsection{Quantum-classical correlations between system and detector}

The joint state of the system allows one to determine the correlations between the detector outcomes and the quantum system. One approach is to define an analogous quantum-classical mutual information between system and detector as,
\begin{eqnarray}
\mathcal{I} = S \left[\int dD \rho_t(D) \right] + S \left[ \Tr\{\rho_t(D)\} \right]- S \left[ \rho_t(D) \right],
\end{eqnarray}
where $S \left[ \cdot \right]$ is either the von-Neumann or Shannon entropy depending on the input. This can be simplified (see Appendix \ref{app2}) as
\begin{eqnarray}
    \mathcal{I}=-\int dD \, S\left[ \tilde{\rho}_t(D) \right] P(D)+S[M_0],
\end{eqnarray}
where $\tilde{\rho}_t(D)=\rho_t(D)/P(D)$ is the state of the system given an outcome $D$.

In general, this requires many matrices $M_n$ for convergence. A comparatively simpler quantity to calculate is the covariance between the signal and a particular system observable $B$, defined as
\begin{eqnarray}
    \rm{cov}(B) &=& \tr \int dD \, B \, D \, \rho(D) - \avg{D} \avg{B} \\
    &=& \sqrt{\sigma} \left[ \tr(M_1 B) -  \tr(M_1) \tr (M_0 B) \right],
\end{eqnarray}
where in the second line (derived in Appendix \ref{app2}), we have shown how this can be computed with only the first two terms. 

\subsection{Fisher information of steady state filtered output current}
For this final quantity, we will focus on the steady state solution where there is no feedback (i.e. $f_p(D) \equiv 0$) and the feedback independent part depends on some parameter $\mu$ i.e. $\mathcal{L}_0 \rightarrow \mathcal{L}_0^\mu$.

To quantify how much information about this parameter is encoded in the steady state filtered measurement record, we compute the Fisher information of $P_\mu(D)$ (which now depends on $\mu$). The Fisher information is defined as
\begin{eqnarray}
    F_I &=& \int dD P_\mu(D) \left| \partial_\mu \ln P_\mu(D) \right|^2.
\end{eqnarray}

Note that $F_I$ refers to the classical Fisher information of the empirical distribution \cite{smiga2023}, where the temporal correlations are discounted, i.e. readings are collated to build a histogram for $P(D)$ in steady state. In practice, we must compute the Fisher information at some fixed value of the parameter. We choose $\mu=0$ i.e. that the parameter is in this neighborhood and so the value of the Fisher information at this value should be indicative. The numerical computation of $\partial_\mu P_\mu (D)$  will be addressed in the next section.

\section{Evolution equation for $M_n$}
We start by using the decomposition of the Liouvillian into the feedback independent part $\mathcal{L}_0$ and the feedback terms $\mathcal{L}_p$.
Inserting our ansatz solution~\eqref{ansatz} in Eq.~\eqref{FP_eq} allows us to write down a system of coupled equations for the operators $M_n$ (see Appendix \ref{app}): 
\begin{equation}\label{FPbasis}
\begin{aligned}
    \dot{M}_m =& \mathcal{L}_0 M_m + \sum_{n=0}^{N-1}  \sum_p \alpha_{n,m,p} \mathcal{L}_p M_n -\frac{\lambda}{2}\left[A,[A,M_m]\right] 
    \\[0.2cm]
    &-\frac{\gamma}{2} \left[ 2 m M_m-\sqrt{\frac{m}{\sigma}}   \{A,M_{m-1}\}  \right], 
\end{aligned}    
\end{equation}
where
\begin{eqnarray}
    \alpha_{n,m,p} = \int_{-\infty}^\infty dD \frac{\mathcal{H}_m(D)}{\sqrt{\sigma^m m!}}  \frac{\mathcal{H}_n(D)}{\sqrt{\sigma^n n!}} \frac{e^{-D^2/2\sigma}}{\sqrt{2 \pi \sigma}}  f_p(D),
\end{eqnarray}
are coefficients determined by the feedback functions $f_p(D)$.
For example, in the case of a Heaviside step function $f_p(D)=\theta(D)$, these coefficients become~\cite{annby-anderssonQuantumFokkerPlanckMaster2022}
\begin{eqnarray}
\alpha_{n,m,p}
&=& \begin{cases} 
      1/2 & n=m \\
      0 & n+m \, \, \rm{even} \\
      \frac{(-1)^{(n+m-1)/2} m!!(n-1)!!}{\sqrt{2 \pi n! m!} (m-n)}
      & n+m  \,\, \rm{odd}
   \end{cases}.
\end{eqnarray}
Similarly in the case of linear feedback $f_p(D)=D$, we get 
\begin{eqnarray}
\alpha_{n,m,p} &=&    \sqrt{\sigma } \left(\sqrt{n+1} \delta
   _{m-1,n}+\sqrt{n} \delta _{m+1,n}\right).
\end{eqnarray}
The matrix elements can be computed analytically for any case where $f_p(D)$ is polynomial in $D$ using recursive relations, see Appendix \ref{app0}. 

These equations can be written more compactly as a single vector equation. We truncate the infinite sum in Eq.~\eqref{ansatz} to a sufficiently large $N$ to ensure a good approximation and define a vector of matrices  ${\vec M} = \left( M_0 \, M_1 \, M_2 \ldots M_{N-1} \right)^T$. Our dynamical equation can now be succinctly expressed as
\begin{equation}\label{Mvec_eq}
 \partial_t \vec{M} = \hat{Q} \vec{M},  
\end{equation}
where $\hat{Q}=\hat{Q}_0 + \hat{Q}_{\rm fb}$ with
\begin{eqnarray}
    \hat{Q}_0 &=& 1_N \otimes \mathcal{L}_0 + \lambda 1_N \otimes \mathcal{D}[A] -\gamma F \otimes 1_d + \frac{\gamma}{2 \sqrt{\sigma}} G \otimes \mathcal{C}_A, \nonumber \\
    \hat{Q}_{\rm fb} &=&  \sum_p \alpha_p \otimes \mathcal{L}_p.
\end{eqnarray}
Here $1_x$ is the identity operator of dimension $x$, 
the system has Liouvillian dimension $d=R^2$, 
$F=\diag(0,1,2,\ldots, N-1)$ is a diagonal matrix, the elements of the matrix $G$ are $\left[G\right]_{i,j}=\sqrt{j} \,\delta_{i,j+1}$, and the elements of the symmetric matrix $\alpha_p$ are
$\left[\alpha_p\right]_{n,m}=\alpha_{n,m,p}$. The total dimension of the matrix $\hat{Q}$ is then $R^2 N$.


\subsection{The case of no feedback}
Equation ~\eqref{FPbasis} simplifies dramatically if there is no feedback present since in this case each $M_m$ depends only on the previous one. 
In the notation of Eq.~\eqref{Mvec_eq}, this manifests as $\hat{Q}_0$ being block lower triangular.  Due to this structure, the steady-state ($\dot{M}_m=0$) can be solved using forward substitution. The equation for $M_0$ becomes 
\begin{equation}
    \Lambda M_0=0,
    \qquad 
    \Lambda := \mathcal{L}_0+ \lambda \mathcal{D}[A],
\end{equation}
which is simply the usual steady state solution for the unconditional dynamics. From $M_0$ one can then recursively solve for the other terms using 
\begin{eqnarray}
(\Lambda-\gamma n 1_d) M_n =  -\frac{\gamma}{2} \sqrt{\frac{n}{\sigma}} \mathcal{C}_A \left( M_{n-1} \right), \label{update}
\end{eqnarray}
for $n=1,2,\ldots$. Note that provided there is a unique solution for $M_0$, this solution is uniquely determined. 

Note that this iterative process could be made even faster by first finding the spectrum 
\begin{equation}
\Lambda= \sum_{j \neq 0} \eta_j \pketbra{x_j}{y_j} ,    
\end{equation}
where $\pket{x_j}$ and $\pbra{y_j}$ are the right and left eigenvectors.  Specifically $\pket{x_0}=\pket{M_0}$, $\pbra{y_0}=\pbra{1_d}$ and $\eta_0=0$. For $n \neq 0$, we can easily compute the inverse as
\begin{eqnarray}
    (\Lambda-\gamma n 1_d)^{-1} = \sum_{j \neq 0} (\eta_j- \gamma n)^{-1} \pketbra{x_j}{y_j} - \frac{1}{\gamma n} \pketbra{M_0}{1_d}.
\end{eqnarray}
The recursion relation can then be expressed as
\begin{eqnarray}
    \pket{M_n} &=& - \frac{\gamma}{2} \sqrt{\frac{n}{\sigma}}  \sum_{j = 0} \frac{\pbra{y_j} \mathcal{C}_A \pket{M_{n-1}}}{\eta_j- \gamma n}  \pket{x_j}  ,
\end{eqnarray}
assuming $\eta_j-\gamma n \neq 0$ for all $j$ and $n$.

Note that this decomposition also allows one to compute the correlations in the measurement current (see Appendix \ref{app3}),
\begin{eqnarray}
    C(\tau) &=& \avg{D(t+\tau)D(t)}-\avg{D(t)}^2\\
     &=&\sigma e^{-\gamma \tau} + \frac{1}{2} \sum_{j \neq 0}  \frac{\gamma  \left(\gamma  e^{\tau  \eta
   _j}+e^{-\gamma  \tau } \eta _j\right)}{\gamma
   ^2-\eta _j^2} \Tr(A x_j) \Tr(y_j^\dagger \{A,M_0\}). \nonumber \\
\end{eqnarray}

\subsection{Parameter derivatives}
When the state $M_n^\mu$ depends on a system parameter (described by $\mathcal{L}_0^\mu$), one can continue to use forward substitution (in the case of no feedback) as before to efficiently compute $P_\mu(D)$.

However, to calculate the Fisher information of the measurement signal, we must extend this to also compute $\partial_\mu M_n^\mu$ for the derivative term $\partial_\mu P_\mu(D)$. We start with the first term $M_0^\mu$. Taking the derivative of $\Lambda^\mu M_0^\mu =0$ (where $\Lambda^\mu=\mathcal{L}_0^\mu + \lambda \mathcal{D}[A]$), we can rewrite this as $\Lambda^\mu (\partial_\mu M_0^\mu) = - (\partial_\mu \mathcal{L}_0^\mu) M_0^\mu$, which we can solve for $\partial_\mu M_0^\mu$. To get the next terms, we can use the iterative update rule Eq. \eqref{update}. Taking derivative of this, provides a new update rule
\begin{eqnarray}
\left(\Lambda^\mu-\gamma n 1_d \right) \left( \partial_\mu M_n^\mu \right)=-\left(\partial_\mu \mathcal{L}_0^\mu\right) M_n^\mu  -\frac{\gamma}{2} \sqrt{\frac{n}{\sigma}} \mathcal{C}_A \left( \partial_\mu M_{n-1}^\mu \right), \nonumber \\
\end{eqnarray}
so given $\mathcal{L}_0^\mu$, $\partial_\mu M_{n-1}^\mu$ and $M_n^\mu$ we can solve for $\partial_\mu M_n^\mu$.

\subsection{Perturbative solution for weak feedback}

We now consider weak feedback control such that our dynamical matrix can be written as $\hat{Q}=\hat{Q}_0 + \epsilon \hat{Q}_{\rm fb}$ where $\epsilon \ll 1$. The solution for our state can then be approximated as a finite sum $\vec{M} \approx \sum_{j=0}^{J_c} \epsilon^{j} \vec{M}^{(j)}$ of $J_c$ corrections. The zeroth order term can be found quickly using forward substitution on $\hat{Q}_0 \vec{M}^{(0)}=0$ as before. The next order corrections can be computed as
\begin{eqnarray}
  \hat{Q}_0 \vec{M}^{(j+1)} &=&  - \hat{Q}_{\rm fb} \vec{M}^{(j)}.
\end{eqnarray}
Note that each step only requires a matrix multiplication by $\hat{Q}_{\rm fb}$ and solving the remaining equation using forward substitution as before.

\section{Numerical examples}

\subsection{Driven qubit}

To start with, we consider the simplest Hamiltonian $H=\Omega \sigma_x$ with a measurement $A=\sigma_z$. In Fig. \ref{fig:compare}, we can see a comparison between the charge resolved approach in red and the statistical collating of trajectories in the orange histograms. We see good agreement between the two at different times during the Rabi oscillations.

Our approach allows us to analytically calculate the summary statistics of the steady state probability distribution $P(D)$. In particular, for this example we get that $\avg{D}=0$ and $\rm{Var}(D)=\frac{\gamma  (\gamma +2 \lambda )}{\gamma ^2+2 \gamma  \lambda +4 \Omega^2}+\frac{\gamma }{8 \lambda}$. The steady state distribution is shown in Fig. \ref{fig:QubitSS}. Note that for increasing measurement strength the outcomes become more localized around $\pm 1$, the eigenvalues of $A$. The value of $\gamma$ is associated with the broadening of these peaks.

