\section{Preliminaries}
Here we present our notation and basic facts used in this paper.

First we give our notation on ordered sets.
We deal with several kinds of orderings.
A \textit{pre-ordering} is a binary relation which is reflexive and transitive.
A \textit{poset} is a pre-ordered set.
%For a poset $P$, its domain is also denoted as $P$, and its order is denoted as $\leq_P$, unless otherwise stated.
A \textit{partial ordering} is an antisymmetric pre-ordering.
%A \textit{pre-linear ordering} is a pre-ordering by which any two elements are comparable.
%A \textit{linear ordering} is an antisymmetric pre-linear ordering.
A \textit{directed set} ($\sigma$-directed set) is a poset in which any finite (countable) elements have an upper bound.


Let $\ordP$ be a poset.
For $p,q \in P$ we write $p <_P q$ if $p \leq_P q$, and $q \not\leq_P p$.
For $p\in P$ let $p\uparrow_{\leq_P}=\{q\in P\mid p\leq_P q\}$ and $p\downarrow_{\leq_P} =\{q\in P\mid q\leq_P p\}$.

Let $\ordP$ and $\ordQ$ be posets and $f$ be a function from $P$ to $Q$.
%$f$ is said to be \textit{order preserving} if $f( p_0 ) \leq_Q f( p_1 )$ whenever $p_0 \leq_P p_1$. $f$ is said to be \textit{strictly order preserving} if $f( p_0 ) <_Q f( p_1 )$ whenever $p_0 <_P p_1$.
$f$ is called an \textit{embedding} if 
$f(p_0) \leq_{Q} f(p_1)$ exactly when $p_0 \leq_P p_1$ for any $p_0, p_1 \in P$.
We say that $f$ is \textit{cofinal} if $f[P]$ is cofinal in $Q$.
$P$ is (\textit{cofinally}) \textit{embeddable} into $Q$ if there is a (cofinal) embedding from $P$ to $Q$.

\begin{comment}
$P$ is said to be \textit{complete} if any bounded subset of $P$ has the least upper bound in $P$.
\begin{comment}

Let $P^*$ be the set of all non-empty upward closed subsets of $P$, which is ordered by reverse inclusion.
Note that $P^*$ is complete since if $X \subseteq P^*$ is bounded, then $\bigcap X$ is the least upper bound.
Moreover, it is easy to check that $f \colon P \rightarrow P^*$ defined by $f(p) = p \uparrow_P$ is cofinal and order preserving.
$P^*$ is said to be the \textit{completion} of $P$.
\end{comment}

