To study the problem of forecasting in the presence of external influence, we choose the domain of stock markets and propose a new approach that uses temporal knowledge graphs (KG). Considering that no such KGs are available, we construct relevant knowledge graphs and propose representation learning techniques to set benchmark results. Our constructed temporal KGs integrate diverse components, including first and second-order stock relationships, corporate events, macroeconomic indicators, events derived from financial statements, financial ratios disclosed during earnings calls, and analyst sentiments. Notably, all of these elements are equipped with time-aware attributes. The capacity to incorporate entities of varying categories, such as Person, Stock Assets, Products, and Alliance, results in an intrinsically heterogeneous knowledge graph, with these heterogeneous entities acting as mediators or connectors, enabling the modeling of complex second-order relationships in the graph. This constructed graph, named Stock Market Knowledge Graph ($\mathsf{SmKG}$), aims to encapsulate all the pertinent information related to stock assets within a given share market. We use this knowledge graph to construct an algorithm that models market movements along with the occurrence of external events that affect them, resulting in a holistic approach to modeling markets. As part of our method, we model the relations in the temporal knowledge graph as events of a heterogeneous Hawkes process on the graph to learn useful embeddings that aid stock price analysis.

