\section{Related Work}
\label{section:Related_Work}
Earlier studies~\cite{zhang2017stock} on stock movement patterns employ Recurrent Neural Networks (RNNs), relying solely on historical stock price data for analysis, which is inadequate due to ignoring the influence of numerous uncertain political-economic factors prevalent in the real world. In recent times, advancements in automated trading strategies have emerged, exploring event-driven and news-driven trading approaches by detecting relevant events in news articles \cite{hu2018listening, zhou2021trade} and social media texts \cite{li2022incorporating}. However, these methods assume that stock movements are independent of one another, restricting the ability to model interactions among different stocks. Further, events extracted from raw texts lack the necessary background knowledge pertaining to the entities and relationships they reference.

A new line of research direction focuses on employing graph-based methodologies to represent pairwise relationships between stocks using metadata such as stock-industry information and various connections between companies, such as supply chain associations and links between CEOs and founders. These methodologies tackle stock movement prediction by formulating it as a node classification problem and enhancing traditional historical price-based prediction models through the utilization of graph-based deep learning approaches. 
\citet{chen2018incorporating} constructs graphs representing shareholder relationships between stocks and utilizes a graph neural network-based model to learn stock movements. Later works \cite{feng2019temporal,matsunaga2019exploring,Qian2024MDGNN} improved the approach by incorporating additional inter-stock relations, such as sector industry connections, supply chain relationships, and inter-executive relationships. They propose a time-aware graph convolutional network that effectively models these inter-stock relations. \citet{ang2022guided} extended this work to incorporate news text data along with inter-stock relations and train their model to generate forecasts of means and volatilities of stock returns over a horizon. \citet{li2022graph} constructs a heterogeneous multi-modal graph that incorporates historical price data, stock news, and technical indicators to model stock trends. \citet{sawhney2020spatiotemporal} and \citet{sawhney2021stock} model higher-order inter-stock relationships by formulating them as hypergraphs. However, despite the efficient modeling of different types of data in isolation, there is currently no existing work that comprehensively captures all of these data modalities in a unified framework.
Incorporating external knowledge has been studied in the context of natural language processing. Previous works have tackled the issue of grounding natural language to knowledge by aligning or combining language and knowledge graph representations~\cite{WANG2023FusingExternalKnowledge}.

In our method, we model relations on the external dynamic knowledge graph as events of a Hawkes process on a graph, using the learned embeddings for improved predictions. Neural Temporal Point Processes~\cite{mei2017neuralhawkes} have been used in a wide variety of applications to model temporal event streams and predict the occurrence and features of future events. Such processes have further been used to model dynamic interactions between different entities, leading to applications in graph edge prediction and relation forecasting in dynamic graphs~\cite{gracious2023neuraltppforforecasting, trivedi2019dyrep, ji2021dynamicheterogenousgraphhawkes}. Edge prediction has also been studied without considering them as temporal processes using latent space models~\cite{gracious2021neurallatentspacemodel} and attention mechanisms~\cite{Xu2020InductiveReprLearningOnTemporalGraphs}.



\iffalse
\subsection{Knowledge Graph Representation Learning}
In this work, we construct temporal knowledge graphs and learn effective dynamic representations of the events to predict some aspects of the future. Traditional Knowledge Graph (KG) tasks primarily focus on making predictions regarding links or entities, and aim to embed entities and relations into a continuous vector space with the goal of revealing inherent semantics. Previous research in this field can be classified into three main groups: Translation models, Bilinear models, and Deep learning models. 
Translation models~\cite{bordes2013translating, wang2014knowledge, sun2019rotate, bordes2013translating} consider relations as translation operations from the head entities to corresponding tail entities, employing distance-based scoring functions to assess the plausibility of a triplet. Bilinear models~\cite{yang2014embedding, nickel2016holographic, trouillon2016complex} incorporate latent semantics of entities and relations within their scoring functions. Deep learning models~\cite{nguyen2017novel} and \cite{yu2021knowledge} leverage deep neural networks to explore the underlying structures of KGs. These models employ techniques such as Convolutional Neural Networks and Graph Neural Networks. KE-GCN \cite{yu2021knowledge}, for example, combines the power of Graph Convolutional Networks (GCNs) in message propagation with the strengths of knowledge embedding methods (translational or bilinear) to effectively represent knowledge graphs. Since our dataset $\mathsf{SmKG}$ is a heterogeneous and temporal KG, our proposed model TA-HKGE utilizes the propagation capabilities of a heterogeneous graph attention network \cite{mo2021heterogeneous} together with a temporally aware translational-based model to model KG embeddings.


\subsection{Static and Dynamic Graph Embedding:}  

Graph embedding methods can be broadly categorized into static and dynamic graph embedding approaches, addressing both homogeneous and heterogeneous graphs.

Early approaches for learning static graph embeddings~\cite{grover2016node2vec, perozzi2014deepwalk} leveraged random walk-based contextual sequences in the skip-gram framework to preserve node relevance. More recent works have focused on graph neural networks (GNNs)~\cite{hamilton2017inductive, kipf2016semi, velickovic2017graph}, which capture neighborhood influence through message passing. However, GNNs fail to account for edge and node heterogeneity, limiting their ability to model the complex semantics in real-world graphs. To tackle heterogeneity, Metapath2Vec \cite{dong2017metapath2vec} and HIN2Vec \cite{fu2017hin2vec} connect nodes based on local proximity using heterogeneous sequences. Further developments have shifted towards heterogeneous GNNs \cite{fu2020magnn}, \cite{zheng2018heterogeneous}, which better exploit both structural and semantic information across the graph. These methods often incorporate heterogeneous attention mechanisms to improve traditional information aggregation \cite{cen2019representation, fu2020magnn, hu2020heterogeneous, wang2019heterogeneous, zhou2021trade}. However, static graph embedding methods overlook the dynamic evolution of interactions, limiting their applicability to dynamic heterogeneous graphs.

In the domain of dynamic graph embedding (also known as temporal network embedding), significant research has emerged in recent years. Early methods, such as CTDNE \cite{nguyen2018continuous}, treat dynamics as temporal bias and use temporal random walks for node embedding. TGAT \cite{xu2020inductive} introduces a temporal encoder that maps continuous timestamps into temporal vectors. To model evolving graphs, recent works divide graphs into snapshots and apply deep auto-encoders or recurrent neural networks \cite{manessi2020dynamic,pareja2020evolvegcn} for learning dynamic embeddings. Efforts to capture both dynamic changes and semantic heterogeneity in dynamic heterogeneous graph embedding have also been explored \cite{hu2020heterogeneous, ji2021temporal, xue2021modeling, yang2020dynamic}. Despite progress, these methods often struggle with the loss of timestamp information in snapshots, which hinders the understanding of graph formation. The Hawkes process, a type of temporal point process, has gained attention for modeling event formation history \cite{lu2019temporal, zuo2018embedding}, but it is primarily designed for homogeneous graphs, making it challenging to handle the heterogeneity in dynamic graphs.

Overall, while significant advances have been made in both static and dynamic graph embedding, the ability to handle dynamic heterogeneous graphs with evolving interactions remains a challenging open problem.
