\textbf{General News} 
News articles were acquired by scraping data from two websites, namely ``Seeking Alpha" for NASDAQ and ``Money Control" for NSE, using the Selenium library in Python\footnote{The news articles are obtained by scraping the webpages https://seekingalpha.com/symbol/\{stock\_ticker\}/news for NASDAQ and https://www.moneycontrol.com/company-article/\{stock\_name\}/news/\{stock\_id\}} for NSE. A total of 220,645 news articles were collected for NASDAQ and 272,808 for NSE. These articles comprise several components, including the headline, body text, publication time, associated stock asset, related sector, and additional taglines, and contain various types of information such as corporate events, earnings reports, analyst and investor sentiments, etc. To identify the events mentioned in the news articles, a rule-based pattern recognition system was employed for a predefined set of events. The identified events are then added to the constructed knowledge graph $\mathsf{SmKG}$ in the form of quadruple $(h, r, t, [\tau_s])$, where $\tau_s$ is the news article publication time.

\textbf{Financial Statements and Ratios}
The financial statements of companies listed under NASDAQ and NSE were extracted by scraping data from the websites ``Macrotrends" and ``Top Stock Research" using the Selenium library in Python\footnote{The financial statements are obtained from www.macrotrends.net and https://www.topstockresearch.com/ for NASDAQ and NSE respectively.}. The collected financial data for each stock consists of various essential components, including the balance sheet, income statement, cash flow statement, and financial ratios. This data is acquired at both annual and quarterly frequencies. Events are derived from the financial statements to identify financial trends by examining whether specific financial parameters exhibit an upward or downward trend. These event trends are then incorporated into the knowledge graph as quintuples, where the interval is defined by the beginning and end of the corresponding financial year or quarter.

\textbf{Macro-Economic Indicators}
Macroeconomic indicators provide a snapshot of the overall economic health of a country, encompassing factors such as GDP growth, inflation rate, unemployment rate, trade balance, and fiscal deficit. This information is obtained from ``Macrotrends" for both the US and India in which the respective share markets NASDAQ and NSE are located\footnote{The macro-economic indicators are obtained from www.macrotrends.net.}. The trends in the indicators are identified as events with intervals defined by the beginning and end of the reported year.

\textbf{Corporate Events}
Corporate events such as dividend announcements and stock splits are acquired from ``Yahoo Finance" for NASDAQ and NSE. These events are represented within the knowledge graph as quadruples, with the timestamp being the date on which the event occurred.

\textbf{Inter-stock Relationship}
The inter-stock relationships can be considered to be of two types: first-order and second-order relationships. If there exists a statement with company $i$ as the subject and company $j$ as the object, then they have a first-order relation. Furthermore, companies $i$ and $j$ have a second-order relation if they have statements that share the same object \cite{feng2019temporal}. For instance, ``Apple" and ``Foxconn" are in different stages in the production of the product ``iPhone". This data was extracted from ``Wikibase," where the knowledge from ``Wikipedia" is stored in the form of triples. A preselected set of relations is identified consisting of $6$ types of first-order relations and $52$ types of second-order relations, which are then filtered from Wikibase.


% First order and second order diagram

\textbf{Sector Classification}
Each stock asset is classified into a four-level hierarchical system: Sector, Industry group, Industry, and Sub-Industry by Global Industry Classification Standard (GICS) for NASDAQ and Macroeconomic Sector, Sector, Industry, and Basic Industry by NSE Indices for NSE. These relations are added as triples with the asset as head, class as tail, and classification level as relation to the constructed knowledge graph.

