\subsection{Baselines}
First, we consider a simple (TRANSF)~\cite{vaswani2017attention} acting on the historical price information $\mathcal{P}$. Next, we consider the Spatio-Temporal Hypergraph Convolution Network (STHGCN)~\cite{sawhney2020spatiotemporal}, which uses a gated temporal convolution over hypergraphs constructed from the stock asset sector classifications, and the Spatio-Temporal Hypergraph Attention Network (STHAN-SR)~\cite{sawhney2021stock}, which models stock dependencies via hypergraphs to capture higher-order relations, with a hyperedge indicating the presence of a first-order or second-order relation. Finally, we consider GCNKG, a two-layer GCN that considers a homogenous directed graph version of the current time snapshot of the constructed knowledge graphs to obtain node embeddings. These are then concatenated with sequential embeddings and used to calculate the prediction scores. The loss function used for training the baselines is the linear combination of pair-wise ranking loss (Equation~\ref{pairwiseloss}) and direction loss (Equation~\ref{dirloss}) defined in Appendix~\ref{appdx:sec:metrics}. All the hyperparameters used for training the baseline and our model are the same.
\subsection{Ablations and Additional Experiments}
\label{sec:ablations}

\paragraph{How much does the sequential embedding as asset features and modeling heterogeneous entities help in model performance?}
We wish to study how much the components of our method affect the model performance. To that end, we empirically compare multiple variants of our model TA-HKGE without Temporal point process. For the first ablation WOTPP, we remove the embeddings obtained from the temporal process modeling and only consider sequential and relational embeddings. For the second variant, LSTM*, we replace the transformer-based sequential embedding in WOTPP with an LSTM. Next, we consider WOSEQ, in which the sequential embedding update for the stock assets as input for the relational embedding layer is removed, along with the embeddings obtained from the temporal point process model. Finally, the temporal point process embeddings and the heterogeneous transformation of entity features are removed to obtain WOHK. Table \ref{change} provides detailed results, from which it is clear that each of the components of our architecture plays an important role in improving the performance of the algorithm.




\paragraph{How well the model perform when the training, validation, and testing time in each phase is increased?}
It is also essential to see how well the model with temporal point process performs when the training, validation, and testing times of each phase are increased since drifts in the stock price over time and changes in the nature of external events can have a negative effect on the model. To measure the extent of this effect, two more training setups are considered. In the first setup ($750$TR), we consider a training period of 750 trading days (3 years) with 75-day validation and a 200-day testing period. In the next setup (1000TR), the training period is 1000 trading days (4 years), with 100-day validation and a 300-day testing period. Table \ref{ablaphase} shows that the performance is affected when the training period is increased. However, the results obtained from the model still beat risk-free returns as indicated by the Sharpe ratio.
