\paragraph{What is the best and worst case scenario in terms of return on investment?}
Since, in practice, it is a difficult task to find the exact value of the closing price of a stock, the best and worst-case scenario in terms of IRR is calculated as well. The best case scenario is when the stock is bought at its low price on day $T$ and sold at its high price on day $T+\Delta$, whereas the worst case is when the stock is bought at its high price on day $T$ and sold at its low price on day $T + \Delta$. Table~\ref{RQ1} shows the obtained best and worst case IRR using our model, including the Temporal point process. It is observed that the variance in the best and worst IRR decreases as the holding period increases. Also, it is to be noted that accurately predicting the closing price is also an important task for real-life applications of this work.



\paragraph{What is the standard deviation of metrics across the 24 phases?}
The market can be bullish or bearish during certain phases, so it is important to look at the standard deviation of the obtained metrics across all the phases. Table \ref{std} shows the standard deviation of metrics across all 24 phases obtained using our model with a Temporal point process. The standard deviation is comparatively lower for the larger datasets S\&P500 and NIFTY500. Also, it can be noticed that the standard deviation is lower when the number of stocks selected is higher.




