\section{Conclusion}
\label{conclusion}
In this paper, for the first time, we try to address the problem of forecasting under external influence. To set benchmarks we built Stock Market Knowledge Graphs ($\mathsf{SmKG}$) that offer a systematic framework for constructing knowledge graphs encompassing comprehensive information about publicly available share market data. Our knowledge graphs are designed to capture the temporal and heterogeneous nature of the data and are specifically constructed for two real-world share markets, namely NASDAQ and NIFTY. By integrating various sources of information, including general news events, financial statements and ratios, macroeconomic indicators, corporate events, inter-stock relationships, and sector classification, our framework provides a unified and consolidated view of the share market.

Furthermore, we introduce a model called TA-HKGE, which leverages the power of heterogeneous graph attention networks, temporal process models, and time-aware translation-based knowledge graph embedding techniques to effectively learn the underlying structures and relationships driving stock prices using the constructed knowledge graph. We empirically demonstrate the validity and effectiveness of our proposed methods on the task of stock ranking based on relevant metrics and showcase the importance and necessity of the various components incorporated in our framework. Overall, our research contributes to the field of share market analysis by providing a comprehensive and efficient framework to solve a forecasting problem by incorporating external knowledge in the form of a temporal knowledge graph.

