\begin{abstract}
The existence of optimal contracts of the principal-agent problem is a long-standing problem. According to the general framework in Cvitani{\'c} et al. \cite{cvitanic2018dynamic}, this existence can be derived from the existence of a classical solution to a degenerated fully nonlinear parabolic partial differential equation problem. In this work we consider the simple case with drift control and quadratic cost function, then prove the existence of classical solution to that PDE.   



\end{abstract}


\section{Introduction}
In the principal-agent problem, a principal delegates an asset to an agent, and the agent will exert efforts in exchange for compensation from the principal. In view of this, the principal will optimize the contract to maximize his own utility. This problem is initially studied in the discrete-time framework. %the problem is analyzed by 
Spear and Srivastava \cite{spear1987repeated} use recursive methods and treat the agent's continuation/promised utility as a state variable. This approach is later extended to the  continuous-time model by Sannikov \cite{sannikov2008continuous}.  %which introduced a continuous-time principal-agent problem with an infinite time horizon. Sannikov's work generalizes the use of the agent's continuation value as a state variable, adapting it to continuous time, which provides an elegant solution approach to this problem.

Cvitani{\'c} et al. \cite{cvitanic2009optimal} is the first to demonstrate the optimal contract in a relatively general continuous-time problem with quadratic cost, using the approach of backward stochastic differential equations (BSDEs). Building on this, Cvitani{\'c} et al. \cite{cvitanic2018dynamic} further develop a systematic method to investigate the general principal-agent problem by reformulating the principal's optimization problem as a standard control problem over both the output process and the promised utility process. The existence of optimal contract is guaranteed if the standard control problem's value function is sufficiently smooth. %with a lump-sum payment over a finite horizon, %again utilizing BSDEs. %Their framework includes the special case of a quadratic cost, providing consistent arguments with Cvitani{\'c} et al. \cite{cvitanic2009optimal}.
However, this regularity cannot be guaranteed in general. To the best of our knowledge, such kind of regularity is only addressed by very special cases (see, e.g., explicit solutions in Cvitani{\'c} et al. \cite{cvitanic2018dynamic} or the one-dimensional case in Possama{\"\i} and Touzi \cite{possamai2024there}). 

The standard control problem will generally be a viscosity solution of the corresponding parabolic partial differential equation (PDE) problem. However, due to the special structure of the principal-agent problem, the corresponding PDE is generally degenerate and fully nonlinear, which does not have a general theory to guarantee the existence of classical solution, and usually only Sobolev regularity is guaranteed. %(See \qsj{Krylov \cite{}}). 

In this paper, by exploiting the special structure of the principal-agent problem with quadratic costs, we will step-by-step construct a classical solution to the parabolic PDE problem. Given the uniqueness of viscosity solution, we actually prove the regularity of the value function, and thus we fill the gap in   Cvitani{\'c} et al. \cite{cvitanic2018dynamic} to verify the conjectured optimal contract is really optimal. 

%the issue of regularity has only been explicitly addressed in Possama{\"\i} and Touzi \cite{possamai2024there}, where they consider a one-dimensional case. Kr{\v{s}}ek and Possama{\"\i} \cite{krvsek2023randomisation} also proves the existence of a smooth solution, but in a weak formulation with a broader admissible set of control, which is only equivalent to the original problem under certain conditions. These conditions, however, must be verified on a case-by-case basis, limiting the applicability of their result to more general settings.

%In this paper, we aim to address this gap by extending the approach outlined in Possama{\"\i} and Touzi \cite{possamai2024there}. We begin by defining a new control problem and subsequently demonstrate that the value function of this new problem is sufficiently smooth. Furthermore, we establish that this value function is equivalent to the original principal's value function, thus resolving the regularity issue in the case of quadratic cost.

The structure of the paper is as what follows. In Sect. \ref{sec model}, we introduce the  principal-agent problem. Sect. \ref{sec auxiliary} presents the (equivalent) standard control problem and proves the equivalence. In Sect. \ref{sec regu w}, we establish the regularity of the value function for the standard control problem. Sect. \ref{conclusion} concludes our paper. %Sect. \ref{sec fur analys} offers further analysis of the optimal contract. Sect. \ref{sec closed form} provides illustrative examples, and Sect. \ref{sec numerical} concludes with a numerical analysis.


\section{Model setup} \label{sec model}
Following the classical literature \cite{cvitanic2009optimal} and  \cite{possamai2024there}, we assume that in the market there is a principal and an agent. Starting from time $t\in [0, T]$, the agent can control the output process $x(s)$, $t\leq s \leq T$ by exerting effort, while the principal's utility will depend on the value of the final output $x(T)$. The principal can observe the output process rather than the agent's effort. In order to incentivize the agent to exert effort, the principal will pre-determine a contract based on the final output to compensate for the agent's effort costs.   

Starting from time $t$, assume that $\left\{ x(s) \right\}_{t\leq s \leq T}$ obeys the stochastic differential equation
\begin{equation}
\begin{cases}
    dx(s) = \sigma\big(x(s)\big) {\lambda}(s) ds + \sigma\big(x(s)\big) d \mathcal{B}(s), & \text{for } s \in (t, T], \\
    x(t) = x,
\end{cases}
\label{dyn_x}
\end{equation}
where $\mathcal{B}(s)$, $s\geq 0$ is standard one-dimensional Brownian motion on a  filtered probability space $\bigg( \mathbb{S}, \mathscr{F},\left\{\mathscr{F}_t\right\}_{t \in[0, T]}, P\bigg)$, %$\sigma\big(x\big)\geq 0$ is a deterministic function of the output $x(s)$, 
\({\Lambda} = \{ {\lambda}(s) \}_{t \leq s \leq T}\) is a process that represents the effort provided by the agent. 
 We assume throughout this paper that the function $\sigma(x): \mathbb{R} \rightarrow \mathbb{R}$ is in {$ C^{5+\alpha}(\mathbb{R}) $} for some $\alpha \in (0, 1)$ and there exists a constant $\sigma_m, \sigma_M > 0$ such that {$\sigma(x) \in [\sigma_m, \sigma_M], \forall x \in \mathbb{R}$. } 


\subsection{Agent's problem} 

Assume that the reward price to the agent from the principal at time $T$ is a function of the final output ${C}\left(x(T)\right)$. We assume the reward function ${C} : \mathbb{R} \rightarrow \mathbb{R}$ is  {continuous}, and satisfies 
\begin{align}\label{growth C}
{|C(x)|\leq c_1(1+|x|)},\ \forall x \in \mathbb{R}
\end{align}    
for some constant $c_1 >0$. %\qsj{Moreover, we require $C$ is a surjection to $\mathbb{R}$. }
Denote this set of functions as $\mathscr{C}$.
 In this paper, we assume that the cost function of exerting effort is of quadratic  form $\frac{c}{2} \lambda(s)^2$, where $c>0$ is a constant.  

With this given contract and the cost function, the agent's problem is to find an optimal effort process $\left\{{\lambda}(s)\right\}_{t\leq s \leq T}$ to maximize the goal function
$$
J_A^{C;{\Lambda}}(x, t)=\mathbb{E}\left[U_A\left(C\left(x(T)\right)\right)-\frac{c}{2} \int_t^T {\lambda}(s)^2 d s\right],
$$
where $U_A$ is agent's utility function, which satisfy the following Assumption \ref{assumption} and 
$$ {|U_A(x)|, |U_A^{-1}(x)| \leq c_0 ( 1 + |x| )}, \ \forall x \in \mathbb{R}.$$
Especially, {we require that $U_A$ is a surjection to $\mathbb{R}$. }
\begin{assumption} \label{assumption}
Utility function $U(x): \mathbb{R} \rightarrow \mathbb{R}$ satisfies {$U(x)\in C^{6+\alpha} (\mathbb{R})$} for some $\alpha \in (0,1)$. Moreover, $U'_i(x) > 0 \geq U''_i(x) $, $\forall z \in \mathbb{R}. $ Additionally, it satisfy %the growth conditions:
\begin{align}
%&|U_P(x)| \leq c_0 ( 1 + e^{c_0{|x|}} ), \quad {|U_A(x)|, |U_A^{-1}(x)| \leq c_0 ( 1 + |x| )},\\
&{\frac{1}{c_0}  e^{-{c_0}{|x|}}  } \leq |U'(x)|, |U''(x)| \leq c_0 ( 1 + e^{c_0{|x|}} ),
%& {|U'_A(x)|} \leq c_0 
\end{align}
for some constant $c_0 >0$. 
\end{assumption}


We require $\Lambda$ should be chosen such that the SDE \eqref{dyn_x} exists a strong solution in $[t, T]$ and $\mathbb{E}\left[U_A\left(C\left(x(T)\right)\right)\right] <+\infty$ to ensure $J_A^{C;{\Lambda}}(x, t)$ well-defined. Denote this admissible set of control as $\mathcal{A}_\lambda(x, t)$. 
Define
$$
v^C(x, t)=\sup _{\Lambda\in \mathcal{A}_\lambda(x, t)} J_A^{C;{\Lambda}}(x, t).
$$
\begin{remark}
 The cost function can be modeled as general functions, however, our current technic only works for quadratic cost. The intuition is that only under quadratic cost function, the optimal contract can only depend on the terminal wealth, while generally the contract should depends on the whole output process $x(s)$, $t\leq s \leq T$. 

