\section{Reduction to a standard stochastic control problem}\label{sec auxiliary}
The above principal-agent problem involves the optimization over a function $C(x)$, which involves technical difficulty. In the seminal work \cite{cvitanic2018dynamic}, this original problem is transformed into a standard stochastic control problem, as the equivalence between these two problems is proved under some assumptions, especially the regularity of the corresponding 
Hamilton-Jacobi-Bellman equation. 

In this section, for reader's convenience, we will first introduce this stochastic control problem, and show the equivalence totally based on PDE. We still need to assume the regularity of the solution, but this regularity will be addressed in the next section.  


Inspired by SDEs \eqref{dyn_x_c} and \eqref{dyn_y_c}, we let $\Lambda = \left\{{\lambda}(s)\right\}_{t\leq s \leq T}$ be a process such that the strong solution  $\left\{ \left(x^{\lambda}(s), y^{\lambda}(s)\right)\right\}_{t \leq s \leq T}$ of the following stochastic differential equation exists in $[t, T]$. 
% \qsj{(Please follow use the cases package when writing two equations..)}
\begin{equation}
\begin{cases}
d x^{\lambda}(s)=\sigma(x^{\lambda}(s)) {\lambda}(s) d s+\sigma(x^{\lambda}(s)) d \mathcal{B}(s), \\
d y^{\lambda}(s)=\frac{c}{2} {\lambda}^2(s) d s+c {\lambda}(s) d \mathcal{B}(s), \\
x^{\lambda}(t)=x, \quad y^{\lambda}(t)=y. 
\end{cases}
\label{auxilliary}
\end{equation}
%Moreover, if $U_A$ is always positive, we require $y^{\lambda}(s)\geq0$, and denote $\mathcal{Y}:= \mathbb{R}^+$; if $U_A$ can take any real value, we denote $\mathcal{Y}:= \mathbb{R}$.  
We denote this admissible set of control as $\mathcal{A}(x, y, t)$ and set
$$
w(x, y, t)=\sup _{\Lambda\in \mathcal{A}(x, y, t)} \mathbb{E}\left[U_P\big(x^{\lambda}(T)-U_A^{-1}(y^{\lambda}(T))\big)\right], \forall x\in \mathbb{R}, y\in \mathbb{R}, t \in [0, T].
$$
We can see that the processes  $\{(x^{\lambda}(s), y^{\lambda}(s))\}_{t\leq s \leq T}$ are closely related to $\{(x^C(s), y^C(s))\}_{t\leq s \leq T}$. However, in this new problem, we treat both of them as controlled processes.

