\section{Regularity of $w(x,y,t)$ and sign of $L_x, L_y$}\label{sec regu w}

In this section, we will verify the assumption in Theorem \ref{thm equi}, and it constitutes the main contribution of our paper. More precisely, we have the following theorem. 
% \qsj{(We have not specified the growth condition in PDE.)}
\begin{theorem}\label{theorem regu w}
Given Assumption \ref{assumption}, if \eqref{equ w visco} allows unique viscosity solution \footnote{see e.g., \cite{capuzzo2018weak} for the proof of the comparison principle.},  then $w(x, y, t) \in C^{4+\alpha, 2+\frac{\alpha}{2}}(\mathbb{R}^2\times [0, T])$ and $$L_y(x, y, t)<0<L_x(x, y, t),\ \forall x, y \in \mathbb{R},\ t \in [0, T].$$ 
\end{theorem}
Our proof of the regularity is based on the special structure of this problem. 
First since we conjecture $w = u$, we expect $y^*(t)$ from the standard control problem should coincide with $y^{C^*}(t) = v^{C^*}(x^{C^*}(t), t)$ from the principal-agent problem for the optimal contract $C^*$. Therefore, fix a constant $l \in \mathcal{L}$, which implies the contract $C^*(x; l)$ from \eqref{equ C*0}, $y^*(t)$ should be a function of $(x^*(t), t)$. Denote this function as $\hat{Y}(x, l, t)$, from \eqref{pde_vc}, we expect 
\begin{align}\label{equ defi hatY0}
\hat{Y}_t + \frac{\sigma(x)^2}{2} \hat{Y}_{xx} + \frac{\sigma(x)^2}{2c} \hat{Y}_x^2 = 0,\ \forall x \in \mathbb{R}, t< T, 
\end{align}
while at time $T$, $\hat{Y}(x, l, T) = U_A(C^*(x; l) )$. In this PDE problem, $l$ can be treated as a constant independent of the operator, and thus the existence of smooth solution $\hat{Y}$ can be derived. Especially, the regularity in dimension $l$ is from the regularity of function $C^*$ on $l$.  

Second, for any $y \in \mathbb{R}$, we expect the existence of $\hat{L}(x, y, t)$, such that 
\begin{align}\label{equ defi hatL1}
y=\hat{Y}(x, \hat{L}(x, y, t), t).
\end{align}
That is, given any $(x, y, t)$, there is always an $l \in \mathcal{L}$, such that $\hat{Y}(x, l, t) = y$. The existence is proved by noticing the monotonicity of $\hat{Y}$ on $y$. 

Third, by differentiating on both sides of \eqref{equ defi hatL1}, we can derive equations about partial derivatives $\hat{Y}_x, \hat{Y}_t, \hat{Y}_{xx}$, and $\hat{L}_x, \hat{L}_y, \hat{L}_t, \hat{L}_{xx}, \hat{L}_{xy}, \hat{L}_{yy}$. Then we can use the partial derivatives of $\hat{L}$ to represent partial derivatives of $\hat{Y}$. We will prove that $\hat{L}_y <0 < \hat{L}_x$. 
By substitute those representations into \eqref{equ defi hatY0}, we can derive the PDE for $\hat{L}$, which is 
\begin{align}\label{equ hat L0}
\hat{L}_t+\frac{\sigma(x)^2}{2}\left(\hat{L}_{x x}-\frac{2 \hat{L}_x}{\hat{L}_ y} \hat{L}_{x y}+\frac{\hat{L}_x^2}{\hat{L}_ y^2} \hat{L}_{y y}\right)-\frac{1}{2 c} \frac{\hat{L}_x^2}{\hat{L}_y}=0, \quad \forall (x, y, t) \in \mathbb{R}^2 \times[0, T).
\end{align}
At time $T$, we have by definition
$$y = \hat{Y}(x, \hat{L}(x, y, T), T) = U_A(C^*(x;  \hat{L}(x, y, T)) ). $$
%where $l'$
From \eqref{equ C*0}, we see at time $T$,
\begin{align}
\hat{L}(x, y, T)= & U_P\big(x-C^*(x; \hat{L})\big)-c \frac{U_P^{\prime}\big(x-C^*(x; \hat{L})\big)}{U_A^{\prime} ( C^*(x;  \hat{L}) )}\\
= &U_P\big(x-U_A^{-1}(y)\big)-c \frac{U_P^{\prime}\big(x-U_A^{-1}(y)\big)}{U_A^{\prime} ( U_A^{-1}(y) )}. \label{equ termin hatL0}
\end{align}

Last, inspired by \eqref{operator_w}, we treat $\hat{L}(x, y, t)$ as a known function and define $\hat{w}(x, y, t)$ as the solution to 
\begin{equation}
\begin{cases} \label{PDE hatw0}
\hat{w}_t+\frac{\sigma(x)^2}{2} \hat{w}_{x x}=\frac{\sigma(x)^2}{2 c} \frac{\hat{L}_x^2}{\hat{L}_y},\quad \text{for } (x, y, t) \in \mathbb{R}^2 \times[0, T), \\
\hat{w}(x, y, T)=U_P\left(x-U_A^{-1}(y)\right),\quad \text{for } (x, y) \in \mathbb{R}^2.
\end{cases}
\end{equation}
The existence, uniqueness, and regularity of $\hat{w}$ is straightforward, since unlike \eqref{operator_w}, the right hand of the PDE operator is a known function. Therefore, fix $y$, we are just solving a non-degenerate parabolic equation. Define $\tilde{L}(x, y, t)=\hat{w}(x, y, t)+c \hat{w}_y(x, y, t)$, and then we can derive from \eqref{PDE hatw0} that 
$$\tilde{L}_t+\frac{\sigma(x)^2}{2} \tilde{L}_{x x}=\frac{\sigma(x)^2}{2 c} \frac{\hat{L}_x^2}{\hat{L}_y}+\frac{\sigma(x)^2}{2} \frac{2 \hat{L}_x \hat{L}_y \hat{L}_{x y}-\hat{L}_x^2 \hat{L}_{y y}}{\hat{L}_y^2}, $$
while from \eqref{equ hat L0}, the right hand side is exactly $\hat{L}_t+\frac{\sigma(x)^2}{2} \hat{L}_{x x}$. Then by noticing \eqref{equ termin hatL0}, we derive 
\begin{align}
\begin{cases}
(\hat{L}-\tilde{L})_t+\frac{\sigma(x)^2}{2} (\hat{L} - \tilde{L})_{x x}  = 0, \quad \text{for } (x, y, t) \in \mathbb{R}^2 \times[0, T), \\
(\hat{L}-\tilde{L})(x, y, T) = 0, \quad \text{for } (x, y) \in \mathbb{R}^2.
\end{cases}
\end{align}
According to the comparison principle, we see $\hat{L}=\tilde{L}$. Hence, from \eqref{PDE hatw0}, $\hat{w}$ is a solution to \eqref{operator_w} and since $\hat{L}_y <0 < \hat{L}_x$ has already been derived in Step 3, we see $\hat{w}$ actually solves \eqref{equ w visco}. From the uniqueness, we see $w = \hat{w}$ is smooth. 

\section{Conclusion}\label{conclusion}
In this paper we prove the existence of classical solution to a degenerated fully nonlinear parabolic partial differential equation problem arising from the principal-agent problem. With this result we fill the gap in the literature to guarantee the existence of optimal contract. The main restriction in our current work is the quadratic cost function, the general case will be left for future research. %Our result is crucial for establishing the equivalence between the principal-agent problem and the  



