\section{Introduction}\label{sec:1}

Tangling is a fundamental characteristic of three-dimensional structures formed by a single or multiple networks. Measuring tangling is essential for understanding various entangled biological and chemical structures \cite{batten_1998,srs_hyde_2008,srs_net_nature}, including coordination polymers \cite{alexandrov2011}, metal-organic frameworks \cite{mof_2001}, covalent organic frameworks \cite{liu2016}, liquid crystals \cite{C4SM01226G}, or DNA origami crystals \cite{dna_tensegrity_triangle, site_directed_placement_DNA_origami}. In fact, some physical and chemical properties of materials are influenced by their entanglement \cite{zhang2022}.

Geometric and topological characterisation of 3-periodic network entanglements has been explored in various contexts \cite{CARLUCCI2003247,  DELGADOFRIEDRICHS20052533, Alexandrov:eo5016, periodic_ent_I, topospro, sym14040822}. A network is, in fact, a particular type of the more general concept of a \textit{graph}. A \textit{graph} is an abstract disjoint collection of loops, infinite threads, and vertices connected by edges.
A \textit{network} is an infinite connected graph, and multiple networks are an infinite graph with finitely many connected components \cite{Eon:ib5036,terminology_MOF_COPolymers}. An \textit{embedding} of a given graph is a geometric realisation of the graph in a given space. 
Geometric properties such as periodicity are, therefore, properties of embeddings and not of abstract graphs. Still, we sometimes abuse the terminology by using terms such as `3-periodic network', for simplicity. Furthermore, unless otherwise mentioned, in this paper we only discuss graphs with 3-periodic embeddings. In Fig. \ref{fig:srs_extended} we show an embedding of the \textbf{srs} network in the Euclidean space $\mathbb{R}^3$. The \textbf{srs} is a regular 3-periodic network of degree-3, that is, three edges share each vertex of the network, highly symmetric and chiral (see RCSR for terminology \cite{rcsr}). Another example of a graph embedding is shown in Fig. \ref{fig:interpenetrating_enantiomorphic_srs_nets_extended}, which is that of two \textbf{srs} networks of opposite handedness.
Two embeddings are said to be ambient isotopic if one can be continuously deformed into the other by moving the structure through space, allowing curves and edges to bend and stretch but not to pass through one another or through vertices.
A fundamental challenge that arises is to determine how tangled an embedding is, up to ambient isotopy, and which embedding is considered the least entangled state \cite{HYDE2011676}. For example, Fig. \ref{fig:interpenetrating_left_handed_srs_nets_extended} and Fig. \ref{fig:0p6on2_to_the_6_extended} show two isotopically distinct embeddings of the same graph, comprising two left-handed \textbf{srs} networks. Intuitively, the embedding of Fig. \ref{fig:interpenetrating_left_handed_srs_nets_extended} is less tangled than the one of Fig. \ref{fig:0p6on2_to_the_6_extended}, and it is this idea that we want to formulate in a rigorous setting.

