Knotting, linking and tangling in a finite graph embedded in space, that is, a collection of finitely many vertices connected by edges, which need not be straight, and finitely many loops, is characterised by the extension to this setting of the mathematical theory of knots and links \cite{R_moves_graph,theta_curves,KAUR2017586}. 
A cycle analysis of an embedding of a finite graph involves examining the knotting and linking of cycles formed by the edges of the embedding \cite{R_moves_graph}. An example is given in Fig. \ref{fig:K6} where two cycles of an embedding of the graph called $K_6$ form a Hopf link (compare with Fig. \ref{fig:hopf_link_a}). There exist some entanglement types that are not captured through a cycle analysis. For example, the embedding $5_1$ of the $\theta$-graph, whose diagram is shown in Fig. \ref{fig:5_1}, is a ravel \cite{ravels} in the sense that there are no knotted or linked cycles, yet the structure is tangled.
Furthermore, unlike links, some graphs, such as the $K_6$ graph, do not possess an embedding that is unlinked \cite{k6_linked}. Extending link invariants to finite graphs therefore requires further consideration, as illustrated by the four types of unknotting numbers described in \cite{KAUR2017586}, which are defined only with respect to certain specifically defined unknotted graphs. For example, the diagram of Fig. \ref{fig:unknotted_theta_graph} represents an unknotted and untangled $\theta$-graph, and the diagrams of Fig. \ref{fig:3_1}, Fig. \ref{fig:4_1} and Fig. \ref{fig:5_1} can all be transformed into the former by changing one crossing of each diagram.

