Cycle analysis has been extended to periodic networks to quantify the entanglement complexity \cite{CARLUCCI2003247,sym14040822,tesselate_decussate}. In this setting, it consists of analysing the knotting and linking of the shortest cycles in the graph embedding, both within a single component and between the disjoint components in the structure, with a difference in this signature indicating different entanglements of the embeddings. It has led to the definition of several notions of entanglement type, such as polycatenation and polyknotting \cite{CARLUCCI2003247}, which are of great interest in classifying network entanglements \cite{batten_1998,Alexandrov:eo5016}.

Various studies, such as \cite{Wells:a01232, Wells:a01308, tesselate_decussate,Delgado-Friedrichs:au5000,Power:ib5087,myf2011_entanglement_graphs,myf_ideal_geo}, address the problem of finding an embedding that may be considered the canonical least tangled form of a single or multiple networks.
For example, in a series of papers on the geometrical basis of crystal chemistry, Wells noted instances of the embedding of two enantiomorphic \textbf{srs} networks \cite{Wells:a01232, Wells:a01308} shown in Fig. \ref{fig:interpenetrating_enantiomorphic_srs_nets_extended}. In fact, this structure can be derived from a single \textbf{srs} network in its barycentric embedding, shown in Fig. \ref{fig:srs_extended}, along with its dual network \cite{Hyde2008ASH}. A barycentric embedding, which is computable with the Systre algorithm \cite{Delgado-Friedrichs:au5000}, is known to avoid unnecessary entanglement.
The configurations shown in Fig. \ref{fig:srs_extended} and Fig. \ref{fig:interpenetrating_enantiomorphic_srs_nets_extended} are also the highest symmetry embeddings \cite{tesselate_decussate,Hyde2008ASH} of the \textbf{srs} network and of two dual \textbf{srs} networks.
Moreover, they minimise the ropelength energy, defined as the ratio of the total length of edges in a unit cell of the embedding to the diameter of the edges, which is computable via the PB-SONO algorithm \cite{myf_ideal_geo}. It is suggested in \cite{myf2011_entanglement_graphs} that an embedding minimising the ropelength energy is untangled.
These observations regarding the \textbf{srs} network and the two dual \textbf{srs} networks may serve as a basis for characterising canonical least tangled embeddings of other networks.

A new measure of entanglement for 3-periodic filament entanglements (\textit{3-periodic tangles}), called the \textit{untangling number}, has been defined in \cite{andriamanalina2025untanglingnumber3periodictangles} at the level of their unit cells, using the diagrammatic representations described in \cite{ANDRIAMANALINA2025109346}. A unit cell is a quotient of a given 3-periodic tangle by a lattice that preserves its periodicity. It is identified with a link embedded in the 3-torus $\mathbb{T}^3 = \mathbb{S}^1 \times \mathbb{S}^1 \times \mathbb{S}^1 $ (where $\mathbb{S}^1$ represents the mathematical circle). A diagram is obtained from a projection of a unit cell along one vector of periodicity. Examples of diagrams and unit cells of 3-periodic tangles are displayed in Fig. \ref{fig:pi_starish_untangling}. Three projections along the three vectors constitute a \textit{tridiagram}. The untangling number of a given 3-periodic tangle quantifies the shortest path between the structure and its least tangled version called \textit{ground state}. This path is constituted of unit cells that possess diagrams that differ by crossing-type changes. An example is given in Fig. \ref{fig:pi_starish_u1_dia} where we display a structure with untangling number 1. We note that there is also studies that cover 2-periodic weavings in a similar context \cite{diamantis2023equivalencedoublyperiodictangles,fukuda2023,fukuda2024constructionweavingpolycatenanemotifs,mahmoudi2024classificationperiodicweavesuniversal}.

