In this paper, we define the notions of diagrams and tridiagrams, as well as the untangling number of graphs, which can be used as a measure of entanglement complexity. The paper is organised as follows: In Sect. \ref{sec:2}, we describe graphs and diagrams of their embeddings based on the work in \cite{ANDRIAMANALINA2025109346}. In Sect. \ref{sec:3}, we define the \textit{untangling number} of graphs, following the work in \cite{andriamanalina2025untanglingnumber3periodictangles}. In Sect. \ref{sec:4}, we discuss some important results regarding the least tangled embeddings. Finally, Sect. \ref{sec:conclusion} is dedicated to the conclusion of this work.

\section{Graph embeddings and their diagrams}\label{sec:2}
In this Section, we describe some diagrammatic representations of graph embeddings in $\mathbb{R}^3$, reminiscent of the work in \cite{ANDRIAMANALINA2025109346} on 3-periodic tangles.\\

We recall that a \textit{graph} is an abstract disjoint collection of loops, infinite curves, and vertices connected by edges. An \textit{embedding} of a graph is a geometric realisation of the graph in a given space. In the following, unless otherwise mentioned, we only consider graphs with 3-periodic embeddings in Euclidean space $\mathbb{R}^3$, that is, the embeddings are periodic along the three directions of space.

\subsection{Unit cells, unit-diagrams and tridiagrams}
Consider a graph $K$ and an embedding $K^e$ of $K$ in Euclidean space $\mathbb{R}^3$.
Due to the periodicity of the embedding, there exists a lattice of translations $\Lambda$ isomorphic to $\mathbb{Z}^3$, which we call lattice of periodicity (crystal lattice), that is naturally associated to it. With a choice of basis, the quotient of $K^e$ by $\Lambda$ yields a structure embedded in the 3-torus $\mathbb{T}^3 = \mathbb{S}^1 \times \mathbb{S}^1 \times \mathbb{S}^1$ that we define as follows.

\begin{definition}
    For an embedding $K^e$ of a graph $K$, we call a \textit{unit cell} the quotient of $K^e$ by a lattice of periodicity, with a chosen basis.
\end{definition}

An example of a unit cell of a graph embedding is shown in Fig. \ref{fig:interpenetrating_enantiomorphic_srs_nets_uc_xyz}.
A parallelepiped delimiting a unit cell can always be rectified to a cube, which we do for simplicity in this paper.

\begin{figure}[hbtp]
    \centering
    \includegraphics[width=0.8\textwidth]{interpenetrating_enantiomorphic_srs_nets_uc_xyz.png}
    \caption{A unit cell of a graph embedding viewed along three directions: the front, the top and the right sides of the cube.}
    \label{fig:interpenetrating_enantiomorphic_srs_nets_uc_xyz}
\end{figure}

To obtain a planar diagram from a unit cell, one can project one face of the unit cell onto a square with identified edges, that is, a 2-torus $\mathbb{T}^2 = \mathbb{S}^1 \times \mathbb{S}^1$. Similarly to usual knots, a diagram is obtained from a projection called \textit{regular}, that needs to respect some rules to avoid ambiguity in aspects such as the nature of a crossing. Some of those rules rely on the notion of transversality, which, for example, requires curves to have non-tangent intersections. See in Fig. \ref{fig:transverse} how the curves can be deformed in space to have transverse intersections.

\begin{figure}[hbtp]
    \centering
    \includegraphics[width=0.75\linewidth]{transverse.pdf}
    \caption{Modifying curves to obtain transverse intersections: Curves intersecting tangentially cause ambiguity in the nature of a crossing, requiring the modification of the intersection type into transverse intersections.}
    \label{fig:transverse}
\end{figure}

Therefore, a \textit{regular projection} is obtained from a unit cell that we assume to be slightly modified to respect the following rules:
\begin{enumerate}
    \item \label{regular_proj:3} every point of the projection has at most two preimages, where only finitely many points, called double points, have exactly two preimages,
    \item \label{regular_proj:2} all double points are transverse,
    \item \label{regular_proj:4} no point intersecting the identified front and back faces of the unit cell is projected onto a double point,
    \item \label{regular_proj:5_6} in the 3-torus, all intersections with the faces are transverse,
    \item \label{regular_proj:7} no double point of the projection lies on the edges of the square delimiting the 2-torus,
    \item  \label{regular_proj:8} no point of the projected curves lies on the corners of the square,
    \item \label{regular_proj:9} in the 3-torus, no point intersecting the identified front and back faces is projected onto the edges of the square,
    \item \label{regular_proj:10_11} no vertex of the graph embedding is projected onto a double point,
    \item \label{regular_proj:12_13} no vertex of the graph embedding belongs to the faces of the cube.
\end{enumerate}

\begin{definition}
    A \textit{unit-diagram} of an embedding of graph, or simply \textit{diagram}, is obtained from a regular projection of one face of a unit cell, with the additional assignment of the following symbols: each double point is assigned a \textit{crossing type} indicating which strand goes over which; a thick dot represents an intersection with the front face, and an open circle represents an intersection with the back face. Furthermore, a set of three diagrams obtained from the projections along the three vectors delimiting a unit cell constitutes a \textit{tridiagram}.
\end{definition}

Fig. \ref{fig:interpenetrating_enantiomorphic_srs_nets_tridia} shows a tridiagram obtained from the unit cell of Fig. \ref{fig:interpenetrating_enantiomorphic_srs_nets_uc_xyz}. Tridiagrams are necessary for converting the full spectrum of transformations in $3$-space into transformations in two-dimensional diagrams, where the crossing information of each diagram is different and equally important.
Unless otherwise mentioned, every tridiagram that we consider in this paper will be obtained from the projections of the front, top, and right faces of a unit cell.

