\subsection{Equivalence of embeddings of graphs}
Equivalence of embeddings of graphs (whether infinite or finite) is usually given through the concept of piecewise linear ambient isotopy (p.l. ambient isotopy), where structures can be deformed in space into equivalent structures without letting the edges pass through each other. Here, however, similarly to the definition of equivalence for 3-periodic tangles given in \cite{ANDRIAMANALINA2025109346}, the definition that we use is a particular case of p.l. ambient isotopy that makes use of unit cells.
Note that there exist infinitely many non-isotopic unit cells representing the same embedding of a graph, as can be seen by the examples shown in Fig. \ref{fig:interpenetrating_enantiomorphic_srs_nets_other_uc}.

\begin{definition}\label{def:equivalence}
We say that two embeddings of a graph are \textit{equivalent} if they are connected by a sequence of embeddings where each two consecutive embeddings possess p.l. ambient isotopic unit cells.
In particular, the periodicity of a given structure is preserved when it is deformed into an equivalent structure.
\end{definition}

\begin{remark}
    We purposefully state Def. \ref{def:equivalence} with less precision than its counterpart for 3-periodic tangles given in \cite{ANDRIAMANALINA2025109346}, to avoid elaborating on details that are beyond the scope of interests in this paper.
\end{remark}

