A \textit{generalised Reidemeister theorem} is a conversion of equivalence of embeddings of (infinite or finite) graphs into equivalence of their diagrams. Proving a generalised Reidemeister theorem for 3-periodic structures would require many difficult steps.
The relations between different unit cells representing the same 3-periodic structure, such as those in Fig. \ref{fig:interpenetrating_enantiomorphic_srs_nets_other_uc}, are the same as those of unit cells of 3-periodic tangles given in \cite{ANDRIAMANALINA2025109346}. No additional work is needed, and details are published in \cite{ANDRIAMANALINA2025109346}.
The problem comes from the conversion of p.l. ambient isotopies of unit cells into transformations of diagrams. First, p.l. ambient isotopies of unit cells need to be converted to simple combinatorial moves known as \textit{$\Delta$-moves} \cite{BurdeZieschangHeusener+2013}. This conversion has been proved for simple curves and finite graphs embedded in $\mathbb{S}^3$, but it is not straightforward for curves in the 3-torus. Indeed, every known proof makes use of unique properties of $\mathbb{S}^3$ that are not true for the 3-torus. Then, a $\Delta$-move needs to be converted to generalised Reidemeister moves in a diagram.
As all of those steps are difficult and beyond the purpose of the paper, we only outline the idea of proof without giving any details. 

P.l. ambient isotopies of unit cells are converted into moves in diagrams called \textit{$R$-moves}, which we list below. They have been created so as to avoid breaking the rules of a regular projection when deforming the curves in space.

