\begin{definition}
    The \textit{$R$-moves} are the set of thirteen moves $R_1,\dots, R_{13}$ that encode p.l. ambient isotopies of unit cells of graph embeddings.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}
    We say that two diagrams $D$ and $D_{\star}$ are \textit{$R$-equivalent} if they are connected by a finite sequence $R$-moves. Furthermore, two tridiagrams $\left\lbrace D_i \right\rbrace_{i=1,2,3}$ and $\left\lbrace D_{\star,i} \right\rbrace_{i=1,2,3}$ are \textit{$R$-equivalent} if they are connected by a finite sequence of tridiagrams where each two consecutive tridiagrams possess two $R$-equivalent diagrams.
More precisely, two tridiagrams $\left\lbrace D_i \right\rbrace_{i=1,2,3}$ and $\left\lbrace D_{\star,i} \right\rbrace_{i=1,2,3}$ are \textit{$R$-equivalent} if there exists a sequence $\left( \lbrace D_{i,k} \rbrace_{i=1,2,3} \right)_{k=0,\dots,n}$ of tridiagrams, for which we have 
 $D_{i,0}  =   D_{i}$, $D_{i,n}  =  D_{\star,i}$, $i=1,2,3$, and for all $k = 1,\dots,n,$ there are $i,j = 1,2,3$ such that $D_{i,k-1}$ and $D_{j,k}$ are $R$-equivalent.

\noindent The $R$-equivalence is denoted by $\sim_R$ both for diagrams and tridiagrams.
\end{definition}

\subsection{Crossing number}
Thanks to the conversion of equivalence of embeddings of graphs to equivalence of their diagrams, the classification of the former can be thought of through the classification of the latter. This is done by using invariants, which are quantities that do not change under changes made to a diagram. We define here the \textit{crossing number}, reminiscent of the crossing number of 3-periodic tangles defined in \cite{ANDRIAMANALINA2025109346}.
Consider an embedding $K^e$ of a graph $K$, and a unit cell $U^e$ of $K^e$, where $K^e$ represents all embeddings of $K$ possessing a unit cell that is ambient isotopic to $U^e$.
To a tridiagram $T = \lbrace D_i \rbrace_{i=1,2,3}$ associated to $U^e$, one can associate a triplet $(a,b,c)$, where $a$, $b$ and $c$ are the numbers of crossings of the diagrams $D_1$, $D_2$ and $D_3$. Such a triplet is referred to as a \textit{triplet of crossings}. The crossing number is defined as follows.

\begin{definition}
The \textit{crossing number of $K^e$ with respect to $U^e$}, denoted by $c(K^e,U^e)$, is the minimum of $c(T) = a^2 + b^2 +c^2$ among all $R$-equivalent tridiagrams.
A tridiagram and its triplet of crossings realising $c(K^e,U^e)$ are respectively called a \textit{minimal tridiagram of $K^e$ with respect to $U^e$}, and a \textit{minimum crossing number triplet with respect to $U^e$}. The \textit{crossing number} of $K^e$ is defined as the minimum of all $c(K^e,U^e)$ among all unit cells $U^e$ of $K^e$, and it is an invariant.
\end{definition}

\section{Untangling number of graphs}\label{sec:3}
To quantify the complexity of tangling in graph embeddings, in this section, we define the concepts of an \textit{untangling number} and a \textit{minimum untangling number} that were first introduced in \cite{andriamanalina2025untanglingnumber3periodictangles} for 3-periodic tangles.\\

The untangling number that we shall define later is an invariant of ambient isotopies of unit cells. Thus, unless otherwise mentioned, every unit cell considered here represents its ambient isotopy class. Consequently, the embedding $L^e$ of a given graph $L$, associated to a unit cell $V^e$, also represents every embedding of $L$ that possesses a unit cell isotopic to $V^e$.

An \textit{untangling operation} is an operation performed on a diagram which consists of inverting the type of a crossing, similar to letting edges and curves pass through each other (just like the unknotting operation of classical knots and links as shown in Fig. \ref{fig:hopf_link_b}). Such an operation does not change the abstract graph, but changes its embedding in most cases. For simplicity, whenever we say that two unit cells $V^e$ and $V^e_{\star}$ are connected by an untangling operation, we mean that there exist two diagrams $D$ and $D_{\star}$ that differ by a single untangling operation, and are respectively connected to diagrams of $V^e$ and $V^e_{\star}$ by series of $R$-moves. These diagrams can be obtained from any of the three projections along the three vectors delimiting $V^e$.

We can now describe the steps necessary to untangle an embedding $K^e$ of a graph $K$ with respect to a chosen unit cell $U^e$. To transform $K^e$ into its least tangled state, perform the following steps:
\begin{enumerate}
    \item\label{step:1} From $U^e$, find a unit cell $U^e_1$ connected to $U^e$ by a single untangling operation. Denote by $K^e_1$ the 3-periodic embedding associated to $U^e_1$.
    \item\label{step:2} Repeat Step \ref{step:1} on $U^e_1$ to obtain $U^e_2$ representing the 3-periodic embedding $K^e_2$, then on $U^e_2$, and so on, until reaching a unit cell $U^e_n$ whose crossing number is the least among all unit cells connected by a series of untangling operations.
    \item \label{step:3} Repeat Step \ref{step:1} and Step \ref{step:2} as many times as possible while changing the diagrams and crossings onto which the untangling operations are applied, to find the least number of untangling operations needed to reach a final unit cell $U^e_n$.
\end{enumerate}

Depending on the choice of crossing changes made in Steps \ref{step:1} and \ref{step:2}, the path to go from $U^e$ to a least tangled unit cell $U^e_n$, is not necessarily unique. This implies variation in the number of iterations in Step \ref{step:3}, as well as the existence of shortest paths.

The \textit{untangling number} with respect to a specific unit cell is the least possible number of untangling operations obtained in Step \ref{step:3}. Every embedding $K^e_n$ of the graph $K$ associated to the unit cell $U^e_n$, of any path to untangling, whether it is one of the shortest paths or not, is called a \textit{ground state} or \textit{least tangled embedding}. Every embedding associated to a unit cell $U^e_n$ of one of the shortest paths to untangling is called a \textit{nearest ground state of $K^e$}.

More precisely, following Step \ref{step:1} and Step \ref{step:2}, one can create a family of embeddings of a graph that are connected via a series of untangling operations and $R$-moves. This leads to the following definition.

\bigbreak

\begin{definition}
   Let $K^e$ be an embedding of a graph $K$ and $U^e$ a unit cell of $K^e$. The \textit{$\mathcal{U}$-family} of $K^e$ with respect to $U^e$, denoted by $\mathcal{U}(K^e,U^e)$, is the set containing $K^e$ and any embedding $K^e_{\star}$ that possesses a unit cell $U^e_{\star}$ connected to $U^e$ by a finite series of untangling operations.
\end{definition}

\bigbreak

Note that any graph embedding $K^e_{\star}$ belonging to $\mathcal{U}(K^e,U^e)$ is always understood to be paired with the unit cell $U^e_{\star}$ that is connected to $U^e$ by a finite series of untangling operations, and not any other unit cell representing $K^e_{\star}$. Within the $\mathcal{U}$-family $\mathcal{U}(K^e,U^e)$, there is a subfamily of embeddings that share the same crossing number, which is the least for all embeddings of $\mathcal{U}(K^e,U^e)$. We define this subfamily and its elements as follows.

\begin{definition}
    Let $K^e$ be an embedding of a graph $K$ and $U^e$ a unit cell of $K^e$. We define the \textit{$\mathcal{G}$-family} of $K^e$ with respect to $U^e$, denoted by $\mathcal{G}(K^e,U^e)$, as the subset of $\mathcal{U}(K^e,U^e)$ consisting of all embeddings having the crossing number that is the least among all embeddings of $\mathcal{U}(K^e,U^e)$. The elements of $\mathcal{G}(K^e,U^e)$ are called the \textit{ground states}.
\end{definition}

\bigbreak

Notice that if $K^e_{\star}$ belongs to $\mathcal{U}(K^e,U^e)$ with respect to the unit cell $U^e_{\star}$, then $K^e$ belongs to $\mathcal{U}(K^e_{\star},U^e_{\star})$ with respect to $U^e$, and thus $\mathcal{U}(K^e,U^e) = \mathcal{U}(K^e_{\star},U^e_{\star})$. Notice also that, by the definition of $\mathcal{G}(K^e,U^e)$, if $K^e_{\star}$ belongs to $\mathcal{U}(K^e,U^e)$ with respect to the unit cell $U^e_{\star}$, then we have $\mathcal{G}(K^e_{\star},U^e_{\star}) =\mathcal{G}(K^e,U^e)$.

We are now in a position to formalise the definition of the untangling number of $K^e$ with respect to the unit cell $U^e$, which can be used as a measure of entanglement complexity for graph embeddings.

\begin{definition}\label{def:untangling_numb_U}
Let $K^e$ be an embedding of a graph $K$ and let $U^e$ be a unit cell of $K^e$. Consider all sequences $(U^e_k)_{k=0,\dots,n}$  of unit cells of embeddings of $K$ belonging to $\mathcal{U}(K^e,U^e)$, satisfying:
\begin{itemize}
    \item $U^e_0 = U^e$,
    \item $\forall k \in \lbrace 1, \dots,n\rbrace$,
    $U^e_k$ and $U^e_{k-1}$ are connected by a single untangling operation,
    \item the embedding associated to $U^e_n$ belongs to $\mathcal{G}(K^e,U^e)$.
\end{itemize}
The \textit{untangling number of $K^e$ with respect to $U^e$}, denoted by $u(K^e,U^e)$, is the least integer $n$ over all such sequences.
\end{definition}

\bigbreak

It follows that the ground states are graph embeddings with untangling number 0. Moreover, every embedding that has untangling number 0 with respect to a chosen unit cell is a ground state.

\bigbreak

It is clear that the untangling number with respect to a given unit cell varies when another unit cell is chosen. To stabilise it, one can take the minimum of all untangling numbers among all unit cells of a given embedding of a graph, leading to the following definition of an invariant.

\begin{definition}\label{def:min_untangling_numb}
The \textit{minimum untangling number} of an embedding $K^e$ of a graph $K$ is defined as the minimum untangling number $u(K^e,U^e)$ over all unit cells $U^e$ of $K^e$.
\end{definition}

\bigbreak

