\bigbreak

The barycentric embedding of a network does not always exist. Indeed, vertices may collapse in the same position or edges may intersect \cite{Delgado-Friedrichs:au5000}. In the latter case, to obtain an embedding, one must move one edge to either side of the other. By doing so, two possibilities for a least tangled embedding are obtained. This means that such networks are the ones that are likely to have various ground states.


