\section{Conclusion}\label{sec:conclusion}
In this article, we describe some diagrammatic representations of graph embeddings and define their untangling number, which can be used as a measure of entanglement complexity, with a focus on 3-periodic tangled networks. These diagrams and the untangling number were first introduced for 3-periodic tangles in \cite{ANDRIAMANALINA2025109346} and \cite{andriamanalina2025untanglingnumber3periodictangles}, respectively.
The untangling number allowed us to quantify the least tangled ground states, defined as the structures with a value of 0. We showed in particular that these ground states coincide for most cases with other characterisations of the least tangled embedding, such as the barycentric embedding, the embedding with highest symmetry or the one minimising the ropelength energy.

 The computation of the untangling number is generally challenging in practice. The process of finding all permutations of crossing changes is difficult. Computational exploration could help in this process, but further notions must first be developed.


