\section{Preliminaries}
\label{sc:pre}

Let $T$ be a tree and $R$ be its vertex. The pair $(T,R)$ is called a {\it rooted tree}, and $R$ is called the {\it root}. For brevity, we will denote $(T,R)$ simply by $T$ and say that $T$ is {\it $R$-rooted} or {\it rooted in $R$}. We only consider trees with odd degrees of vertices. A vertex $u$ is a {\it descendant} of a vertex $v$ in $T$, if the path between $u$ and $R$ contains $v$. A descendant of $v$ which is also its neighbour is called a {\it child} of $v$. For every vertex $v$ of $T$, we denote by $T_v$ the subtree of $T$ induced by all descendants of $v$ and $v$ itself rooted in $v$. The {\it height} of $T$ is the maximum distance between its root and its leaf. Let us denote the height of $T_v$ by $h_T(v)$ and call it the {\it height of $v$}.


We call a rooted tree $T$ {\it $k$-ary}, if all vertices other than leaves have $k+1$ neighbours: for consistency with the definition of the majority dynamics, we let the root of a $k$-ary tree to have exactly the same degree as other non-leaf vertices. We call a $k$-ary tree {\it perfect}, if all its leaves are at the same distance from the root. %For consistency with the definition of the majority dynamics, we let the root of a perfect $k$-ary tree to have exactly the same degree as other non-leaf vertices.
A (perfect) 2-ary tree is also called a {\it (perfect) binary tree}. In particular, the root of a (perfect) binary tree has degree 3.\\

%We call the tree binary, if every vertex is a leaf or has exactly two children. Therefore, every vertex except of the root has degree one or three and a root has degree two if it is not the only vertex in the tree.


% [$t$-stable, $t$-semi-stable]

Let us now run the majority dynamics on a rooted tree $T$ with an arbitrary initial vector of opinions $\xi_0$. Let $t\in\mathbb{Z}_{\geq 0}$. Let us call a vertex $u$ {\it $t$-stable}, if for every $s\geq t$ with the same parity as $t$, $\xi_{s}(u)=\xi_{s+2}(u)$ (here we assume that $0$ is even). The vertex is {\it $t$-stationary}, if it is $t$-stable and $(t+1)$-stable.

%{\color{blue}Let us call $u$ {\it $t$-semi-stable}, if either it is $t$-stable, or for the minimum $t_0\geq t$ with the same parity as $t$ such that $\xi_{t_0}(u)\neq\xi_{t_0+2}(u)$, we have that $\xi_s(v)=\xi_{s+2}(v)$ for all children $v$ of $u$ and all $s\in[t-1,\ldots,t_0-1]$ with the same parity as $t-1$. In particular, if the children of $u$ are $(t-1)$-stable, then $u$ is $t$-semi-stable. In addition, a vertex can only be semi-stable but not stable if exactly half of its children have value $1$ at time $t-1$.\\}

