\begin{proof}
Let us note that if $h_T(v) = 1$, then $v$ is strongly 0-stable and, hence, $v$ is 1-close to stability. So, we may assume that $h_T(v) \geq 2$.
%The second statement of Claim~\ref{cl:weak_calculations} follows from the definition. So, let us prove the first statement. 
 Let us first describe the proof strategy. For a child $u$ of $v$, we will define three specific events expressed only in terms of initial opinions on $V(T_u)$. We will show that if $v$ is not 1-close to stability, then for some child of $v$ at least one of the three events holds. Then, we will bound probabilities of these three events and use these bounds to derive the statement of Claim~\ref{cl:weak_calculations}.\\

% Next, for every child $u$ of $v$ and every of the three respective events, we will show an event with a good enough probability bound that is expressed only in terms of initial opinions on $V(T_u)$ and that implies the former event. In the last part of the proof we argue that the above probability bounds imply Claim~\ref{cl:weak_calculations}.

