In the present work, our main topic is to investigate the weighted endomorphisms on $C_0(X_A)$ dual to the shift map, where $X_A$ is the \emph{generalized countable Markov shift} and its spectral radius. We discovered that the existence of these endormophisms is equivalent to being able to continuously extend the shift map $\sigma$ to the whole space, where we presented several examples of existence and absence of such an extension. In particular, we present two classes of shift spaces, namely \emph{single empty-word} and \emph{periodic renewal} classes of shift spaces, were the aforementioned endomorphisms do exist. Moreover, for both classes, the set of empty words $E_A$ is invariant in the sense that $\sigma(E_A) \subseteq E_A$, because the shift map on this set is a cycle. In addition to that, we explicitly determined a formula for the spectral radius of these endomorphisms for the generalized renewal shift, which was inspired by the work of B. K. Kwa\'sniewski and A. Lebedev \cite{KwaLeb2020}, where they computed explicitly the spectral radius for the compact standard Markov shift (finite alphabet) in terms of the maximal value over the invariant measures attains on products on values that depends on finite sequences of the standard Markov shift $\Sigma_A$ and those measures. Our formula required another compact space of finite sequences of a space $\{0,1\}^{N_a}$, which depends on the element of $a \in \spann \, \mathcal{G}_A \subseteq C(X_A)$, where $\mathcal{G}_A$ is a chosen set of (projection) generators of $C(X_A)$. The spectral radius of the rest of the elements of $C(X_A)$ is obtained via limits of sequences of elements of $\spann \, \mathcal{G}_A$. Using ergodic optimization arguments for the renewal shift and coercive potentials from \cite{Bissacot2014}, in the particular case where $a \in \spann \, \mathcal{G}_A$ has a decomposition in terms of the generators that does not contain the identity of $C(X_A)$, we may take the maximum value attained over the invariant measures on a compact transitive subshift of $\Sigma_A$, that is, a finite alphabet whose the matrix restricted to this alphabet corresponds to a strongly connected symbolic graph.

This paper is organized as follows.




Besides the well-known importance of the theory of differential equations in several areas of the natural sciences, it is essential to consider the class of functional differential equations. In traditional examples of this class of equations, it is necessary to investigate equations containing weighted shift operators \cite{Antonevich}. Furthermore, we can consider weighted shift operators not only for their potential applications but also because they possess a rich and complex mathematical structure. For example, some works obtained characterizations of compact weighted operators \cite{Kamowitz,Jamison} and, more recently, the study of the spectrum of such operators \cite{Dong}.

The study of the spectral properties of such weighted shift operators can be carried out using all the tools available from operator algebras. In fact, it is possible to relate the original problem to an endomorphism in an algebra invariant under the shift operator embedded in a space of operators. In this way, our aim is to combine tools from algebra, the study of dynamical systems, and ergodic theory to investigate this fundamental problem. More precisely:



In Section \ref{sec:Exel_Laca_GCMS}, we present the Exel-Laca algebra $\mathcal{O}_A$ and the set $X_A$ as a generalization of the Markov shift space $\Sigma_A$, which corresponds to the spectrum of the maximal commutative $\mathrm{C}^*$-subalgebra $\mathcal{D}_A$ of the Exel-Laca algebra. We provide a faithful representation of the Exel-Laca algebra in $\mathfrak{B}(\ell^2(\Sigma_A))$. In addition, we show a perspective on the elements of $X_A$ as configurations on Cayley trees, determined by a finite word called the \emph{stem} and a subset of the alphabet called the \emph{root}, where the last one is only used for elements that correspond to finite words. We briefly explain the topology of $X_A$ via \emph{generalized cylinder sets} and present the shift map, the latter defined in the open subset of $X_A$ corresponding to the non-empty words. 

Later, in Section \ref{sec:Representations for D_A}, we explicitly show the Gelfand transformation between the $\mathrm{C}^*$-algebras $C_0(X_A)$ and $\mathcal{D}_A$. For the Cuntz-Krieger algebras (finite alphabet), the Gelfand transformation connects the operators $S_w S_w^*$ to the characteristic function of the cylinder $[w]$, and it is extended to the whole C$^*$-subálgebra $\mathcal{D}_A$ onto $C(\Sigma_A)$. Our construction shows the similarities and the differences between the finite and infinite alphabet cases. We wrote it in a friendly way that could be useful for the community in future research.

Section \ref{sec:weighted_endomorphisms} starts with the basic definitions and results concerning the weighted endomorphisms we used in this paper. Later in the same section, we define a function $\alpha_0$ on the generators of $\mathcal{D}_A$ to $\mathfrak{B}(\ell^2(\Sigma_A))$ that is the candidate to extend to the $*$-endomorphism $\alpha$ on $C_0(X_A)$ dual to the shift dynamics $\sigma$. In fact, we proved that such an extension exists (equivalently, the image of $\alpha_0$ is contained in $C_0(X_A)$) if and only if $\sigma$ has a continuous extension to $X_A$. In this case, $\alpha$ is dual to the extension of $\sigma$. By the Gelfand representation, we have equivalently $\alpha(f) = f \circ \sigma$, $f \in C_0(X_A)$. The first example of a transition matrix that does not provide such an endomorphism is presented in this section.

Now, in Section \ref{sec:examples}, we introduce two classes of generalized Markov shifts: one with a single empty word and the other with a finite number of $n$ empty words. We showed that these two classes admit a well-defined endomorphism $\alpha$ $C_0(X_A)$ that is dual to $\sigma$. Some examples are presented to justify some of the choices in the statement of the definition of the classes above, as well as many examples of matrices whose $X_A$ belong to the classes above. These classes are not disjont, since the renewal shift is in both families, but they are also non-proper each other because the lazy renewal shift and the pair renewal shift are exclusive elements of each class above, respectively. In particular, the column-finiteness condition implies that the set $E_A$ of empty-word configurations is invariant, and we explicitly show how the dynamics on this set acts.

Next, in Section \ref{sec:Spectral Radios on Markov shift}, we focus on the generalized renewal shift in the final section, and it is dedicated to characterize the spectral radius of the weighted endomorphisms on $C(X_A)$, associated to the dual endomorphism $\alpha$ dual of the shift map, whose existence is a consequence of the previous section. We present a formula for the spectral radius $r(a\alpha)$, $a \in \spann \, \mathcal{G}_A$ of the weighted endomorphisms associated with this renewal shift space. Since the map $a \mapsto r(a\alpha)$ is continuous, we may calculate $r(a\alpha)$ for every $a \in C(X_A)$. Furthermore, we show here that, in the particular case where $a \in \spann \, \mathcal{G}_A$ has a decomposition as a linear combination that does not include the identity, then the formula depends on invariant measures on the compact transitive standard Markov subshift $\Sigma_A(\mathcal{A})$ where the matrix $A$ is restricted to a finite alphabet $\mathcal{A}$.

