    \begin{equation*}
        \nu(B)=\nu(B\cap\Sigma_A)+\nu(B\cap E_A)=\nu(\Sigma_A)\frac{\nu(B\cap\Sigma_A)}{\nu(\Sigma_A)}+\nu( E_A)\frac{\nu(B\cap E_A)}{\nu(B\cap E_A)},
    \end{equation*}
where the second identity holds provided that $\nu(\Sigma_A)$ and $\nu(E_A)$ are non-zero. On the other hand, $\sigma^{-1}(\Sigma_A) = \Sigma_A$ and $\sigma^{-1}(E_A) = E_A$ for $\nu$-almost every point, so the functions $\nu_1=\frac{\nu(B\cap\Sigma_A)}{\nu(\Sigma_A)}$ and $\nu_2=\frac{\nu(B\cap E_A)}{\nu(B\cap E_A)}$ are invariant probability measures and therefore belong to $\text{Inv}(\Sigma_A,\sigma)$ and $\text{Inv}(E_A,\sigma)$, respectively. If $\nu(\Sigma_A) = 0$ or $\nu(E_A) = 0$, the result is straightforward.
