\begin{lemma}\label{Lemma: Beta infty. ergodic optimization for potentials on the extended line}
Let $f:\Sigma_A\to \mathbb{R}\cup\{-\infty\}$ be a function defined as $f=\ln|\widehat{a}|$, for some $a=\sum_{k=1}^{N_a}\lambda_ke_{\gamma^k,F_k}$ such that $\beta_f=-\infty$. Then, for all $\mathcal{A}$ such that $A\vert_{\mathcal{A}\times\mathcal{A}}$ is irreducible
\begin{equation*}
    \beta_f=\max_{\mu\in\text{Inv}(\Sigma_A(\mathcal{A}),\sigma)}\int f d\mu
\end{equation*}
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
On one hand, we have $\int f d\mu=-\infty$ for any $ \mu\in\text{Inv}(\Sigma_A,\sigma)$ as a consequence of $\beta_f=-\infty$. On the other hand, let $\text{InvPer}(\Sigma_A(\mathcal{A}),\sigma)$ be the set of periodic invariant measures. Since the set of periodic invariant measures is dense in that of ergodic measures, then
    \begin{align*}
        \max_{\mu\in\text{Inv}(\Sigma_A(\mathcal{A}),\sigma)}\int f d\mu=\max_{\mu\in\text{Erg}(\Sigma_A(\mathcal{A}),\sigma)}\int f d\mu=\max_{\mu\in\text{InvPer}(\Sigma_A(\mathcal{A}),\sigma)}\int f d\mu.
    \end{align*}
    Let $\mu$ be a measure in $\text{InvPer}(\Sigma_A(\mathcal{A}), \sigma)$. As the periodic orbit in $\Sigma_A(\mathcal{A})$, where $\mu$ is supported, is also a periodic orbit in $\Sigma_A$, we can consider $\mu$ as a measure in $\text{Inv}(\Sigma_A(\mathcal{A}), \sigma)$. Hence, we conclude what was intended.
\end{proof}



