The Hubble tension refers to the significant discrepancy between early-universe estimates of the Hubble constant \( H_0 \), derived from CMB observations assuming the spatially flat \(\Lambda\)CDM model, and higher values obtained from local measurements using Cepheids and Type Ia supernovae \cite{Planck:2018vyg, Verde:2019ivm, Freedman:2024eph, Li:2025ife,Li:2025lfp, CosmoVerse:2025txj}. Since spatial curvature affects the relation between distances and redshift, even small deviations from flatness can shift cosmological parameter estimates and potentially alleviate this tension. Consequently, theoretical efforts to resolve the tension also explore modifications to dark energy, interactions with dark matter, additional relativistic species, and early dark energy \cite{Artymowski:2020zwy, DiValentino:2021solutions, Artymowski:2021fkw, Ben-Dayan:2023rgt, Ben-Dayan:2023htq, Dainotti:2023future, Khalife:2023hubble}. Clarifying the source of the Hubble tension remains essential for testing the consistency of the standard cosmological model and identifying possible new physics.

