\section{Introduction}

Financial risk estimation is more than a regulatory checkbox. It's a foundational tool for ensuring market stability and investor confidence (\href{https://doi.org/10.1111/j.1540-6261.1952.tb01525.x}{Markowitz, 1952}; J.P. Morgan, 1996). Yet, when markets enter turbulent regimes, traditional risk models often fall short. Value-at-Risk (VaR) and Expected Shortfall (ES) methods, for instance, typically assume Gaussian or other parametric return distributions (\href{https://doi.org/10.21314/JOR.1998.001}{Hull \& White, 1998}), which systematically underestimate extreme losses. Events like the 2008 global financial crisis (\href{https://doi.org/10.1257/jep.23.1.77}{Brunnermeier, 2009}) and the COVID-19 crash (\href{https://ssrn.com/abstract=3574447}{Baker et al., 2020}) exposed the fragility of these assumptions—especially in the tails.

\medskip
\noindent The core challenge is that financial returns violate the independent and identically distributed (i.i.d.) assumptions baked into many statistical learning techniques. Real-world returns are heteroskedastic, exhibit regime shifts, and often have heavy-tailed distributions (\href{https://doi.org/10.1080/713665670}{Cont, 2001}). GARCH-type models (\href{https://doi.org/10.2307/1912773}{Engle, 1982}; \href{https://doi.org/10.1016/0304-4076(86)90063-1}{Bollerslev, 1986}) can capture some of this volatility clustering, but they still depend on pre-specified distributions and lack reliable coverage guarantees for interval forecasts.

\medskip
\noindent In recent years, conformal prediction has gained traction as a powerful distribution-free framework for quantifying uncertainty. Classical conformal methods provide finite-sample coverage guarantees without assuming a particular data distribution (\href{https://doi.org/10.1007/b106715}{Vovk et al., 2005}). However, these guarantees rest on the assumption of exchangeability, a condition often violated in time series data with temporal dependencies. Several recent works attempt to address this, extending conformal methods to sequential or temporally dependent settings (\href{https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2010.09107}{Xu \& Xie, 2021}; Stankeviciute et al., 2021), but many lack adaptability or practical robustness in financial domains.

\medskip
\noindent This paper closes that gap. We introduce \textbf{Temporal Conformal Prediction (TCP)}, a real-time, adaptive framework for constructing valid prediction intervals in financial time series. TCP combines modern quantile regression with a rolling-window conformal calibration mechanism and an adaptive thresholding scheme governed by a decaying learning rate. Crucially, this architecture bridges statistical inference and machine learning, combining the theoretical rigor of conformal methods with the flexibility of data-driven quantile estimators. The result is a framework that ensures robust empirical coverage even in the presence of non-stationarity, volatility clustering, or abrupt market shifts.

\medskip
\noindent We benchmark TCP across three major asset classes, \textbf{equities (S\&P 500)}, \textbf{cryptocurrency (Bitcoin)}, and \textbf{commodities (Gold)}, and compare it against established models like GARCH, Historical Simulation, and standard Quantile Regression. Our results reveal that TCP consistently achieves narrower prediction intervals while maintaining competitive coverage: for example, delivering 88.5\% coverage for Bitcoin with sharper bounds compared to 95\% coverage by QR at significantly wider intervals. Most importantly, TCP operates in an online fashion, making it readily deployable in real-world risk management systems, including those aligned with Basel III/IV regulatory standards. In short, TCP fills a critical need: it offers a practical, interpretable, and distribution-free alternative for forecasting financial risk—precisely when conventional models fall short.


\medskip
\noindent We organize the paper as follows. Section~\ref{sec:related} surveys related work in risk modeling and conformal methods. Section~\ref{sec:math} lays out the TCP theory. Section~\ref{sec:methodology} details our empirical setup. Section~\ref{sec:result} presents the results. Finally, Section~\ref{sec:conclusion} wraps up with practical takeaways and future directions.
