\section{Related Work}
\label{sec:related}

\subsection{Traditional Financial Risk Models}

Quantitative risk management traces its roots to Markowitz’s portfolio theory (\href{https://doi.org/10.1111/j.1540-6261.1952.tb01525.x}{Markowitz, 1952}) and was formalized around the Value-at-Risk concept with J.P. Morgan’s RiskMetrics in 1996 (Morgan, 1996). In practice, three main VaR estimation paradigms have dominated:

\smallskip
\noindent
\textbf{Parametric Approaches:}
Models like Risk Metrics and its GARCH-style extensions assume a parametric return distribution, often Gaussian or Student’s t, with volatility updated via exponentially weighted moving averages (EWMA) (Morgan, 1996; Jorion, 2007). These frameworks are easy to implement and computationally light (Dowd, 1998), but they habitually understate extreme losses when returns depart from the assumed distribution.

\smallskip
\noindent
\textbf{Historical Simulation:}
This non-parametric method ranks past returns and takes empirical quantiles as risk estimates (\href{https://doi.org/10.2307/20076262}{Danielsson \& de Vries, 2000}). It sidesteps distributional assumptions but hinges on the notion that history repeats itself rendering it vulnerable to regime shifts. A widespread improvement, filtered historical simulation, first scales returns by their estimated volatility (e.g. from a GARCH model) before ranking (Barone-Adesi et al., 1999).

\smallskip
\noindent 
\textbf{Monte Carlo Simulation:}
By simulating thousands of scenarios from a specified stochastic model, potentially including stochastic volatility, jumps, or heavy tails, Monte Carlo methods can capture complex portfolio payoffs (\href{https://doi.org/10.1007/978-0-387-21617-1}{Glasserman, 2004}). Yet they reintroduce model risk via the need to choose and calibrate the underlying distributional dynamics.

\medskip
\noindent Beyond VaR, Conditional Value-at-Risk (CVaR) was proposed as a coherent alternative that remedies VaR’s lack of subadditivity (\href{https://doi.org/10.21314/JOR.2000.038}{Rockafellar \& Uryasev, 2000}). Regardless of the estimator, however, traditional approaches often fail backtests during market crises (\href{https://doi.org/10.3905/jod.1995.407942}{Kupiec, 1995}; \href{https://doi.org/10.2307/2527341}{Christoffersen, 1998}), highlighting their blind spot when tail events dominate.

\subsection{Advanced Time Series Models}

To tackle volatility clustering and heavy tails, the GARCH family became foundational: \href{https://doi.org/10.2307/1912773}{Engle’s ARCH (1982)} and \href{https://doi.org/10.1016/0304-4076(86)90063-1}{Bollerslev’s GARCH (1986)} models capture time‐varying variance, with later extensions like EGARCH for asymmetric volatility (\href{https://doi.org/10.2307/2938260}{Nelson, 1991}) and GJR-GARCH for leverage effects (\href{https://doi.org/10.1111/j.1540-6261.1993.tb05128.x}{Glosten et al., 1993}).  Extreme Value Theory (EVT) then offered a principled way to model tails (\href{https://doi.org/10.1214/aos/1176343003}{Pickands, 1975}; \href{http://dx.doi.org/10.1016/S0927-5398(00)00012-8}{McNeil \& Frey, 2000}), but it demands large samples and careful threshold selection.  Copula-based approaches (Sklar, 1959) separate margins from dependence, enabling flexible multivariate risk modeling, though picking the right copula often remains subjective.

\subsection{Machine Learning in Finance}

Machine learning has injected fresh momentum into risk estimation.  Quantile regression (\href{https://doi.org/10.2307/1913643}{Koenker \& Bassett, 1978}) directly targets conditional quantiles without assuming a distribution; modern variants include neural quantile regression (Taylor, 2000) and gradient-boosting forests (\href{http://jmlr.org/papers/v7/meinshausen06a.html}{Meinshausen, 2006}).  Deep architectures, especially LSTM networks have also shown promise in capturing complex temporal dependencies in return series (\href{https://doi.org/10.1016/j.ejor.2017.11.054}{Fischer \& Krauss, 2018}).

\subsection{Conformal Prediction Theory}

Conformal prediction (\href{https://doi.org/10.1007/b106715}{Vovk et al., 2005}) provides distribution-free, finite-sample prediction intervals by measuring how “conforming” each new observation is to past data.  Classical conformal methods assume exchangeability, which breaks down in time series.  Recent advances introduce adaptive calibration to handle distribution shift (\href{https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2106.00170}{Gibbs \& Candès, 2021}) and extensions specifically for temporal data (\href{https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2010.09107}{Xu \& Xie, 2021}; Stankeviciute et al., 2021), laying the groundwork for truly robust, time-aware uncertainty quantification.

