\vspace{1em}

\noindent Table~\ref{tab:results} presents the performance of the Temporal Conformal Predictor (TCP) compared to three widely used baselines namely Quantile Regression (QR), GARCH, and Historical Simulation on three distinct asset classes: equities (S\&P 500), cryptocurrency (Bitcoin), and commodities (Gold).

\paragraph{Equity (S\&P 500):}
TCP produces the \emph{sharpest} intervals (2.66) while achieving a competitive coverage rate of 86.1\%. Although QR and Historical Simulation provide higher coverage (95.1\% and 93.1\%, respectively), they do so with much wider intervals (4.95 and 5.06), potentially overestimating risk. GARCH underperforms on both coverage and sharpness. TCP thus emerges as a method that balances reliability and informativeness in stable markets.

\paragraph{Cryptocurrency (Bitcoin):}
TCP shows its strength in high-volatility regimes, maintaining coverage of 88.5\% with significantly narrower intervals (9.61) compared to QR (10.31) and Hist (18.06). GARCH again lags behind in both dimensions. These results demonstrate TCP’s ability to adjust dynamically to volatile market conditions without producing overly conservative forecasts.

\paragraph{Commodity (Gold):} 
For gold returns, TCP achieves the narrowest intervals (2.20) while keeping coverage at 88.1\%. In contrast, QR and Hist widen the intervals to over 4.02, and GARCH delivers the lowest coverage at 83.7\%. TCP therefore proves to be an efficient method for capturing tail behavior even in relatively steady commodity markets.

\subsection{Overall Analysis}

Across all three asset types, TCP consistently generates \textbf{tighter} prediction intervals than its competitors, with \textbf{coverage rates that remain within acceptable bounds}. This reflects TCP’s adaptive calibration mechanism and learning-based quantile estimation, which together enable robust performance even in the presence of volatility clustering or structural regime shifts.

\medskip
\noindent The results validate TCP as a strong alternative to traditional parametric models and pure machine learning baselines. While QR achieves the highest coverage in most cases, it does so at the cost of sharpness. TCP, by contrast, offers a more practical and interpretable trade-off, making it a promising candidate for financial forecasting and risk estimation in both stable and turbulent markets.
%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%  

\section{Conclusion and Future Work}
\label{sec:conclusion}

In this paper, we introduced Temporal Conformal Prediction (TCP), a flexible and adaptive framework for generating prediction intervals in financial time series. Our method combines the strengths of quantile regression with conformal calibration, and crucially adapts over time using a decaying learning rate to correct for miscalibration. Unlike traditional models, TCP does not assume stationarity or rely on rigid parametric forms, making it well-suited to handle the complex, noisy, and often non-i.i.d. nature of financial returns. Unlike existing adaptive conformal approaches 
(\href{https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2106.00170}{Gibbs et al., 2021)}, TCP explicitly leverages temporal exchangeability and decaying learning rates to handle financial time series' unique nonstationarity. This makes it particularly suited for regulatory applications where robustness to market regime shifts is critical.

\medskip
\noindent We benchmarked TCP against three widely used alternatives: standard Quantile Regression (QR), GARCH models, and Historical Simulation. The results, across equities (S\&P 500), cryptocurrency (Bitcoin), and commodities (Gold), show that TCP achieves a strong balance between coverage and sharpness. While QR slightly outperforms in coverage, it does so with significantly wider intervals. TCP consistently produces more efficient forecasts without compromising reliability, especially in volatile settings like crypto markets.

\medskip
\noindent One of the key takeaways is that TCP offers a principled way to navigate the \textbf{coverage–sharpness tradeoff}, which is central to real-world forecasting applications. In practice, users may prefer slightly lower coverage if it results in tighter intervals, especially in finance, where interpretability, regulatory compliance, and capital efficiency matter. TCP’s distribution-free design aligns with Basel III/IV and SEC stress-testing requirements, which mandate robust risk assessments under varying market conditions. By providing valid intervals without parametric assumptions, TCP could streamline compliance workflows while improving capital allocation efficiency. By design, TCP fills a growing need for \textbf{distribution-free, adaptive forecasting tools} in financial modeling, a high-impact domain given increased scrutiny around risk assessments and regulatory robustness.


\subsection{Future Work}
While our study focuses on univariate daily returns, several promising extensions remain. First, TCP can be generalized to multivariate or portfolio‐level forecasting by modeling joint or conditional quantiles. Second, richer quantile estimators, such as neural networks or advanced tree‐based methods using additional market features, may further improve interval sharpness and adaptability. Third, it would be valuable to benchmark TCP against volatility models that leverage realized‐volatility inputs, for example true Realized-GARCH fitted on intraday realized variances or Realized-GARCH variants using rolling-window proxies. Finally, deploying TCP in live trading simulations or real-time risk‐management systems will help assess its operational robustness, latency, and scalability. We leave these directions for future exploration.  


