\section{Existing GP frameworks}
\label{sec:existing}

Many GP implementations are currently available either for general or specific purpose. The specific purpose tools are often the implementation of the many variations of GP, provided by the authors, that present new techniques, representations, or mechanisms that improves over the state-of-the-art. For example, SRBench~\cite{de2024srbench,la2021contemporary}) provides instructions and installation scripts for many SR algorithms.
The general purpose tools aim at providing a simple experience for practitioners to apply GP and other algorithms to their own applications without needing experience with a specific programming language. Even though they implement different algorithms, when it comes to GP, most of them only implements a single representation targeting regression and classification problems.

%\textcolor{red}{TODO: Highlight shortcommings such as multimodality (representations and problem domains) as well as lack of an HPO interface}

The Java-based Evolutionary Computation Research System (ECJ)~\cite{DBLP:conf/gecco/ScottL19, DBLP:conf/gecco/Luke17} was first introduced in 1998 and it is still under constant development. This toolkit provides support to different evolutionary algorithms and nature-inspired algorithms for different problem domains. Specifically for GP, its main implementation is a tree-based GP with support to strongly-typed programs and automatic definition of functions. 
As this is a mature software, it provides an extensive documentation allowing community contribution of extensions, which led to a support to Cartesian GP, gene expression programming, and the Push language. With the complexity of a general and mature software, the learning curve to introduce new ideas and contributing to the source-code can be steep for the inexperienced programmer, limiting its use for %when one wants to create 
quick prototyping of new ideas.

Another mature software is GPLab~\cite{silva2003gplab}, written in Matlab and supporting tree-based GP. As its stronger points, it provides an easy-to-use experience inside the Matlab environment requiring a single command to solve a problem with the default hyperparameters. It also provides extensive customization of the different aspects of its internal implementation of the GP algorithm together with the possibility of customizing the fitness function and the list of nonterminals, allowing the adaptation to different domains. Despite the original version requiring the proprietary software Matlab, it provides support to the free alternative Octave. The limitations of this tools lie on the limitations of Matlab and Octave that focus on array-based programming which can make it difficult to implement different representations without decreasing the running time performance.

DEAP~\cite{DEAP_JMLR2012,de2012deap} is a Python framework for evolutionary computation that supports a subset of evolutionary algorithms while still allowing the inclusion of new algorithms through its extensive support to customization. It implements a tree-based GP as a default with a support to the symbolic regression task. This toolbox is also customizable allowing the user to specify the implementation of each component of the algorithm, including the nonterminals, fitness function, and evolutionary algorithm operators. This framework requires the user to write a few lines of code to run even a simple example. Despite the provided customized experience, it is not trivial to adapt to different representations.

A recently released Python framework is EC-KitY~\cite{eckity2022git,eckity2023} which implements a Genetic Algorithm and a tree-based Genetic Programming. Since it is a new framework (released in 2022), support to different algorithms and representations is under development. The main highlight of this tool, in contrast to DEAP, is the support to two different modes: an sklearn-compatible mode which provides a simple interface to the main algorithms providing a simpler experience to the user, and a standard mode providing more customization to the algorithm. 

Genetic Engine~\cite{espada2022data} is a Python implementation of a hybrid between strongly-type genetic programming (STGP) and grammar-guided genetic programming (GGGP). The main focus of this tool is to allow the incorporation of doman knowledge through type annotations and the customization of the grammar. As with EC-KiTY, it provides an sklearn-compatible mode for easy of use targeted to regression and classification problem domains.

Unlike these frameworks, Tinyverse GP main focus is on benchmarking and comparing different representations under different domains while keeping the main genetic programming implementation as simple as possible. With the minimalistic philosophy in mind, we intend to keep the project small, avoiding generating a monolithic application, that can be easily used for prototyping, benchmarking, and didactic purpose (i.e., used as a reference code). As it will be detailed in the next section, the main module provides the abstract specification that each representation must support together with a default implementation of the common components shared among the representations.

