\paragraph{\bf Tree-based Genetic Programming}
% cartesian (cgpp), tree (tgp), linear (lgp), stack (sgp), grammar (ggp)
Tree-based Genetic Programming (hereby, TGP)~\cite{koza1990genetic,koza1994genetic,Koza:1992:GPP:138936}, also referred to as Koza-style GP, represents its genotype using a tree data structure with variable arity. In this representation, each internal node represents a non-terminal with $1$ or more children, and the leaf nodes represent the terminals.

The main advantage of this representation lies in its simplicity and straightforward algorithms for evaluating, perturbing, and combining solutions. The evaluation procedure is a recursive function that applies the operator represented by the root to the recursive call of the evaluator in each of its children. One example of mutation operator is illustrated in Fig.~\ref{fig:subtree-mutation}, called subtree mutation, in which a subtree is selected and replaced by a new randomly generated tree. The corresponding crossover operator, called subtree crossover, selects a subtree from the first parent and replaces it with a random subtree extracted from a second parent (see Figure~\ref{fig:subtree-cx}).


\paragraph{\bf Graph-based and Cartesian Genetic Programming}
Graph-based genetic programming as a GP subfield naturally evolved from the developments taken place in tree-based GP starting from the mid to late 1990s by the proposal of different graph-based representation models for GP-driven synthesis of programs. Graph-based models for GP can therefore be considered an extension of traditional (single) tree-based GP, as these models allow more connectivity between nodes and enabling the synthesis of multiple-output topologies. Graph-based GP enabled novel and applications in contemporary domains such as circuit design~\cite{DBLP:conf/ddecs/FiserSVS10, Sekanina2011} neural architecture search~\cite{10.1162/evco_a_00253, 10.1162/evco_a_00253} and policy learning~\cite{DBLP:conf/gecco/WilsonCLM18}.

The most popular representative of graph-based GP is Cartesian Genetic Programming (CGP)~\cite{mi-th-fo-97a,miller:1999:ACGP} that has been continuously studied and extended since its inception. CGP represents a genetic program by using an acyclic directed graph which overcomes combinational limitations inherent in tree-based data structures. The genetic programs of a population are encoded in a \textit{genotype} which is decoded to a corresponding phenotype.

Originally, the programs were encoded with a rectangular $n_\textnormal{r}$  $\times$ $n_\textnormal{c}$ grid of nodes but later work concentrated on a representation that only used one row which led to the predominant use of a vector representation of CGP. In CGP, \textit{function nodes} execute functions, defined in the function set, on the given input values. Each function node is therefore associated with a non-terminal symbol. 

%The decoding routine virtually splits the genome into groups of genes, where each of these groups represents a vertex of the graph, except the last one, which serves as as a reference to the outputs or sinks of the graph. A function node is encoded with two different types of genes: 1) the so called \textit{function gene}, that stores the corresponding function index of the function set and 2) the \textit{connection genes}, that stores references of the inputs of the node. The number of connection genes of each function node is fixed based on a predefined arity setting $n_\textnormal{a}$ which depends on the maximum arity of the function set. The decoding of function nodes is embedded in a backward search routine that is performed for all output genes to decode the respective paths. The number of inputs $n_i$, outputs $n_o$ and function nodes $n_f$ remains the same and therefore naturally also the size of each genotype in the population. The total number of genes can be calculated as $N = n_\textnormal{r} * n_\textnormal{c} * (n_\textnormal{a} +1) + n_\textnormal{o}$ integer numbers. However, although the length of the genotype cannot be changed, the length of the is variable on the phenotype level, which allows a certain level of flexibility of the representation model. 

%A minimal example of the backward search is illustrated in Figure~\ref{cgp_decoding} for the single-row representation. It first considers the program output and then decodes all referenced function nodes in the genotype until the inputs are reached. Merely only active function nodes are therefore processed during the decoding. The genotype as illustrated in Figure~\ref{cgp_decoding} is grouped in such a way that the first (underlined) gene of each group refers to the function index in the function set and the non-underlined genes refer to the respective input connections. Function nodes, that are not linked in the genotype, remain inactive and are illustrated in gray dashed lines. To control the connectivity of the graph, a parameter called levels-back $l$ is commonly used to constrain the maximum index of a previous function node from which a function or output node can receive its inputs. 
%CGP is commonly used with a ($1+\lambda$) evolutionary algorithm (EA) and a selection strategy called \textit{neutrality}, which leverages the idea that the adaption of a neutral genetic drift mechanism (NGD) can considerably contribute to overcome local optima or stagnation on plateaus. NGD extends the classic selection mechanism of the traditional $(1+\lambda)$ selection strategy by first identifying individuals that have the same fitness as the normally selected parent, and one of these individuals with equal fitness is then selected uniformly at random. NGD can be understood as a random walk in the region of neutral neighbors having equal fitness in the population and is similar to non-strict selection as applied in random local search. Each generation, a new population is created with the selected parent from the previous population and the $\lambda$ offspring obtained from genetic variation. An example of the ($1+\lambda$)-EA variant used in CGP is provided in Algorithm~\ref{cgp_algorithm}. A point mutation operator is commonly used for breeding which varies gene values of the genotype within the respective (valid) range by chance. The mutations caused by this operator can variate the genotype on the functional and the connectivity level depending on which type of gene is selected to being mutated. CGP has been found to perform most efficient when a small population is used in combination with a high number of function nodes~\cite{DBLP:conf/ki/KaufmannK17}. It is commonly used without recombination but recombination operators have been proposed in recent time\cite{DBLP:conf/ppsn/Kalkreuth22}. 

