\subsection{The Zen of TinyverseGP}

Following our main motivation, we take a first step towards a unified framework by proposing a prototype that follows a modular design and already supports tree- and graph-based GP as well as provides interface for benchmarks from three remarkably distinct problem domains. We follow the philosophy that our proposed modular and object-oriented design enables flexibility for further extensions. TinyverseGP is meant to be a community-driven project that connects people on various levels inside and outside of the GP field. With the proposal of our framework we intend to develop towards more comprehensive evaluation results with the goal of achieving a larger perspective of the performance and capabilities of GP. To unify different methodologies in an efficient manner we follow the philosophy of balancing a middle way between minimalism, flexibility and usability to establish a lightweight architecture that makes extensions easy to handle. As such, the three points of our philosophy can be summarized as:

\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Inclusion:} TinyverseGP is a Python-based framework. Python can be considered an inclusive language as it is highly versatile and provides a broad range of tools that can be used to implement many EC and ML methodologies in a straight-forward way. Moreover, since Python is a popular language in the ML/AI field it will appeal to a large number of people to participate in the project. Making extension easy by providing reusable and interpretable code structures.
    \item \textbf{Simplicity:} As Python itself can be considered a language that promotes simplicity, this goes hand in hand with our philosophy of establishing a broad framework that follows a minimalistic approach. We facilitate low levels of complexity by focusing on minimalistic modules and therefore maintain a lightweight framework. We focus on only using what is really needed.
    \item \textbf{Flexibility:} With TinyverseGP we pursue the development of adaptable and scalable structures with the intention of pursuing unification of methodologies and problems that differ widely.
\end{itemize}

\begin{algorithm}
\caption{Illustration of the (1+$\lambda$)-EA variant with neutral genetic drift (NGD)}
\begin{algorithmic}[1]
\small
\State \texttt{Initialization($\mathcal{P}$)} \Comment{Initialize parent individual}
\Repeat \Comment{Until termination criteria not triggered}
\State $\mathcal{Q} \gets$ \texttt{Breeding($\mathcal{P}$)} \Comment{Breed $\lambda$ offspring by mutation}

\State \texttt{Evaluation}($\mathcal{Q}$) \Comment{Evaluate the fitness of the offspring}

\State $\mathcal{Q}^\mathnormal{+} \gets$ \texttt{Best}($\mathcal{Q},\mathcal{P}$) \Comment{Get individuals which have better fitness as the parent}
\State $\mathcal{Q}^\mathnormal{=} \gets \texttt{Equal($\mathcal{Q},\mathcal{P}$)}$ \Comment{Get individuals which have the same fitness as the parent}
\LineComment{If there exist individuals with better fitness}
\If {$|\mathcal{Q}^\mathnormal{+}| > 0$} 
\LineComment{Choose one individual from $\mathcal{Q}^\mathnormal{+}$ uniformly at random}
\State $\mathcal{P} \gets \mathcal{Q}^\mathnormal{+}$[r], $r \sim U$[0, $|\mathcal{Q}^\mathnormal{+}| - 1$] 
\LineComment{Otherwise, if there exist individuals with equal fitness }
\ElsIf {$|\mathcal{Q}^\mathnormal{=}| > 0$}
\LineComment{Choose one individual from $\mathcal{Q}^\mathnormal{=}$ uniformly at random}
\State $\mathcal{P} \gets \mathcal{Q}^\mathnormal{=}$[r], $r \sim U$[0, $|\mathcal{Q}^\mathnormal{=}| - 1$] 
\EndIf
\Until {$\mathcal{P}$ \textit{meets termination criterion}} 
\State \textbf{return} $\mathcal{P}$ \Comment{}
\end{algorithmic}
\label{cgp_algorithm}
\end{algorithm}

